               & "le"
               & LF
               & "    corner: point = zeroed -- fine: both fields are floa"
               & "ts"
               & LF
               & "    -- facing_now: compass = zeroed -- error, write 'nor"
               & "th'"
               & LF
               & "    -- p: ptr u32 = zeroed -- error, no zero image"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- Does the type have a zero image? It does when all-zero"
               & " bits are a valid value of it, which is what lets a surr"
               & "ounding struct or array be zeroed. Pointers and `any` ha"
               & "ve none, because there is no null, and neither does a fu"
               & "nction address. A struct or an active variant payload th"
               & "at contains a function address must spell that field out"
               & " instead of using whole or trailing `zeroed`."
               & LF
               & "- Does the type accept the word? Only when its zero has "
               & "one obvious reading. Numbers, `bool`, ranges that contai"
               & "n zero, and aggregates of those accept it. Named value s"
               & "ets do not: write the name. A distinct type [0650] has i"
               & "ts base's zero image, so a record holding one zeroes fin"
               & "e, but an identity such as a user id has no obvious zero"
               & ", so write `user_id(0)` rather than `zeroed` for it."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Where the destination gives the context, `zeroed` writes"
               & " the complete zero image as one value, as the assignment"
               & " to `buffer` does."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A packed struct ([0730]) is a raw image: `zeroed` is all"
               & "owed even when an encoded field's zero pattern has no na"
               & "me. Reading that field then traps; copying the image kee"
               & "ps its bits."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Library code has a narrow cousin, `uninit`, which leaves"
               & " a private array field unwritten. It is not another spel"
               & "ling of `zeroed` and cannot initialize a stand-alone arr"
               & "ay.",
            when 550 =>
               "`zeroable` is the first of those two properties as a con"
               & "cept, so generic code can require it. Concepts come in ["
               & "1240]; for now read one as a named property a type can h"
               & "ave."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    -- compiler-owned; a program does not repeat this de"
               & "claration"
               & LF
               & "    zeroable: type = concept (t: type)"
               & LF
               & "    end zeroable"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Only the compiler knows a type's bit patterns, so only t"
               & "he compiler supplies its conformances; it is the one con"
               & "cept like that, and a program cannot declare a conforman"
               & "ce to it. The family holds:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- the enabled scalars;"
               & LF
               & "- a fixed array exactly when its element is zeroable, ev"
               & "en at length zero;"
               & LF
               & "- a struct exactly when its all-zero shape, recursively,"
               & " has a zero image;"
               & LF
               & "- a distinct type exactly when its base is zeroable."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Atoms, functions, pointers and slices are not in it. It "
               & "is a closed list, not reflection over arbitrary types."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "This is how a container that needs a filler value for st"
               & "orage it has not used yet says so, instead of pretending"
               & ": see `cleared` at [1350] and the reason at [0510].",
            when 570 =>
               "A slice `[]t` is a pointer and a length. It copies nothi"
               & "ng. `mut` inside the type says its elements may be writt"
               & "en."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    mut cells: [4]f32 = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0]"
               & LF
               & "    view: []f32 = cells[0..<2] -- read-only elements"
               & LF
               & "    edit: []mut f32 = cells[0..<2] -- writable, since ce"
               & "lls is mut"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "You can pass an array straight to a slice parameter; it "
               & "lends a slice of the whole array. A named array lends it"
               & "s own storage. An array literal is built in the caller's"
               & " frame for the duration of the call, so a list of values"
               & " needs no binding of its own."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    added: (values: []f32) -> (sum: f32) ="
               & LF
               & "        sum = 0.0"
               & LF
               & "        for value in values do"
               & LF
               & "            sum += value"
               & LF
               & "        end for"
               & LF
               & "    end added"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    both_sums: () -> (both: f32) ="
               & LF
               & "        both = added(grid) + added([1.0, 2.0])"
               & LF
               & "    end both_sums"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A literal lends read-only elements only, so it cannot go"
               & " to a `[]mut t` parameter.",
            when 580 =>
               "`[]` is the empty slice: length zero, and a `for` over i"
               & "t runs no iteration. It is never null. Its base is the l"
               & "owest positive address aligned for the element type, 1 f"
               & "or bytes and 4 for a `u32`, and since indexing checks th"
               & "e length first, that base is never dereferenced.",
            when 590 =>
               "Fixed arrays are also Landin's vector type: arithmetic o"
               & "n numeric arrays works element by element. There is no s"
               & "econd type with the same shape and different rules."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    sum_four: (values: [4]f32) -> (sum: f32) ="
               & LF
               & "        sum = 0.0"
               & LF
               & "        for value in values do"
               & LF
               & "            sum += value"
               & LF
               & "        end for"
               & LF
               & "    end sum_four"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    array_example: () -> (sc: [4]f32, s: f32) ="
               & LF
               & "        va: [4]f32 = [1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0]"
               & LF
               & "        vb := va * va"
               & LF
               & "        sc = va * 2.0 -- a scalar on either side is fine"
               & LF
               & "        s = sum_four(vb) -- ordinary function, not a red"
               & "uction builtin"
               & LF
               & "    end array_example"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- `+`, `-`, `*`, `/` and unary `-` work on numeric array"
               & "s; `%`, `+%`, `-%` and `*%` on integer arrays."
               & LF
               & "- Two arrays must have the same element type and length."
               & " A scalar of the element type is applied to every elemen"
               & "t; an array of another length is not."
               & LF
               & "- Each operand is evaluated once, left to right, as a co"
               & "mplete value before any element is computed. So `a += a`"
               & " and other overlapping assignments see the old values, n"
               & "ot their own earlier writes."
               & LF
               & "- Elements go in ascending index order under the ordinar"
               & "y scalar rules for traps, wrapping and floating point. N"
               & "othing is reassociated, so `sum_four` adds in order star"
               & "ting from positive zero."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Arrays do not compare, not even with `==` or `<>`: whole"
               & "-array equality and per-element masks are both plausible"
               & ", so write the function you mean."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The cost is visible in the type: `[4096]f32 + [4096]f32`"
               & " needs a 16 KB result and may keep copies of its operand"
               & "s as well. Vector instructions are an optimisation, neve"
               & "r needed for a correct result.",
            when 600 =>
               "Landin has no single string type. It has three distinct "
               & "views over plain storage:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "| type | view over | holds |"
               & LF
               & "|---|---|---|"
               & LF
               & "| `utf8` | `[]u8` | validated shortest-form UTF-8 |"
               & LF
               & "| `utf16` | `[]u16` | UTF-16 code units |"
               & LF
               & "| `cstring` | `ptr u8` | bytes up to a NUL; no length |"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A quoted or raw literal can be any of the three, and is "
               & "`utf8` when nothing says otherwise. `lenof` on `utf8` an"
               & "d `utf16` counts code units, not characters. The three t"
               & "ypes stay distinct from each other and from their backin"
               & "g types through calls, structs and generics."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Every literal has one trailing zero code unit, which sli"
               & "ce lengths leave out, and equal literals of the same wid"
               & "th may share one read-only copy. A `cstring` literal is "
               & "valid UTF-8; a `cstring` that comes from C promises only"
               & " readable bytes up to its first NUL, with its encoding u"
               & "nchecked."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Converting. Conversion between text and bytes is the ord"
               & "inary explicit conversion, and these are all there are:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "| from | to | what it does |"
               & LF
               & "|---|---|---|"
               & LF
               & "| `[]u8` (read-only) | `utf8` | validates, and traps on "
               & "invalid UTF-8 |"
               & LF
               & "| `utf8` | `[]u8` (read-only) | nothing: same base, same"
               & " length |"
               & LF
               & "| `cstring` | `[]u8` (read-only) | scans to the first NU"
               & "L |"
               & LF
               & "| `cstring` | `utf8` | scans to the first NUL and valida"
               & "tes |"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "There is no conversion from a pointer to `cstring`, none"
               & " with a writable result, and none for `utf16`. An empty "
               & "result keeps its source's base and origin."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Slicing. `utf8` and `utf16` slice by range, as `word[3.."
               & "3]` in [0610] does. The result is the same text type, re"
               & "ad-only, over the same storage."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- The `usize` bounds count bytes or UTF-16 code units, n"
               & "ot characters."
               & LF
               & "- Both bounds must fall where a Unicode scalar starts; a"
               & " bound out of range or inside an encoding traps."
               & LF
               & "- A half-open range may end at the length; an inclusive "
               & "range takes the whole scalar that starts at its upper bo"
               & "und."
               & LF
               & "- A `cstring` has no length and cannot be sliced."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Traversal. `for c in text` yields each Unicode scalar as"
               & " a `u32` [1320]; on a `cstring` it stops before the firs"
               & "t NUL. Traversal declares no error, so text from C is va"
               & "lidated as it is decoded and malformed encoding traps, e"
               & "ven inside `unchecked`."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "**`core/text`.** When invalid text is input to handle ra"
               & "ther than a bug, `text.from_bytes` and `text.from_c` val"
               & "idate the same way but fail with `invalid_text` instead "
               & "of trapping; call them before traversing. The module als"
               & "o has exact byte equality, substring search, decimal par"
               & "sing and bounded writing into a buffer you supply. It ne"
               & "ver normalizes, allocates or silently truncates."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "On plain `[]u8`, `core/text` counts positions as byte of"
               & "fsets of the opaque type `text.position`, with `first`, "
               & "`at_end`, `next`, `advance`, `offset`, `byte` and `slice"
               & "`. Reading a byte at the end fails with `past_end`, and "
               & "a slice keeps its source's origin.",
            when 610 =>
               "Indexing a `utf8` gives the codepoint there as a `u32`, "
               & "the type a character literal [0250] and a traversal [132"
               & "0] already give it. The index type picks the operation:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- a `usize` counts codepoints from the start, so `word[2"
               & "]` is the third one; the compiler finds it by scanning;"
               & LF
               & "- a `core/text.position` is a byte offset, decoded direc"
               & "tly in O(1)."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    word: utf8 = ""\u{e9}t\u{e9}"""
               & LF
               & "    last := word[2] -- 233, the u32 of "
               & Character'Val (195)
               & Character'Val (169)
               & ", found by scanning"
               & LF
               & "    same := last == '\u{e9}' -- true"
               & LF
               & "    bytes: utf8 = word[3..3] -- "
               & Character'Val (195)
               & Character'Val (169)
               & " again, at byte 3: two bytes, no copy"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A count past the end traps, and so does a position at th"
               & "e end or on a continuation byte. Neither index type conv"
               & "erts implicitly to the other."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The codepoint is decoded, so it is a value, not a place:"
               & " `addr word[2]` and `word[2] = 'e'` are errors. To get i"
               & "ts bytes, take a one-codepoint range as [0600] describes"
               & ": its bounds count bytes, and the result is still `utf8`"
               & ". A `utf8` converted to `[]u8` loses codepoint indexing "
               & "and has the ordinary slice operations instead.",
            when 1410 =>
               "Every `.ldn` file directly inside one directory belongs "
               & "to one module, and those files see each other's declarat"
               & "ions without importing anything. Everything is private t"
               & "o its module unless it is marked `public`. There is no s"
               & "eparate interface file."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    geometry/area.ldn"
               & LF
               & "    geometry/shapes.ldn"
               & LF
               & "    geometry/solid/volume.ldn"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Here `geometry` is one module of two files, and `geometr"
               & "y/solid` is a second module."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Fine print:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- The compiler reads a module's files in bytewise filena"
               & "me order. Other files are ignored, and an empty director"
               & "y is a legal empty module."
               & LF
               & "- Declaration order never affects what is visible. File "
               & "order does fix identities, the order of diagnostics and "
               & "the order of active `linker.library` directives [1590]."
               & LF
               & "- An importer can name public functions, bindings, atoms"
               & ", types and concepts. A variant case is public when its "
               & "type is."
               & LF
               & "- A public declaration may mention a private one. The pr"
               & "ivate identity flows through the public surface, but an "
               & "importer cannot name it.",
            when 1420 =>
               "`import net/http` finds a directory `net` holding a dire"
               & "ctory `http`, under the import roots the compiler was gi"
               & "ven, and binds the last segment, `http`, as a namespace "
               & "in this file."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    import net/http"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The roots are searched in order, and the first that has "
               & "the directory wins; a later root is not merged in. From "
               & "that directory the compiler takes every source file, not"
               & " recursively, and follows their imports. A subdirectory "
               & "is a module of its own, reached by its own path. Each mo"
               & "dule is loaded once, so import cycles are legal."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "After the import, `http.get`, `http.response` and `http."
               & "drawable` name that module's public declarations. Qualif"
               & "ication works wherever a value, type, type application, "
               & "concept, conformance, error atom or match case is named."
               & " The first dot after the namespace selects a public decl"
               & "aration; later dots are ordinary value selections. The n"
               & "amespace itself is not a value, type or place."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A path segment is a plain identifier: lowercase letters,"
               & " digits and underscore, not starting with a digit. Lower"
               & "case, because a case-sensitive and a case-insensitive fi"
               & "lesystem must agree on what a name is, and the directory"
               & " entry must have exactly that spelling even on a case-in"
               & "sensitive host. An identifier, because the last segment "
               & "becomes a name in your file, so a hyphen would need an a"
               & "lias every time. The separator is always `/` in source, "
               & "on every host.",
            when 1430 =>
               "`as` gives the namespace another name, for when two impo"
               & "rts collide."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    import net/http as h"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "This binds `h` only, not `http`. `as` is a contextual wo"
               & "rd, so outside an import it is an ordinary identifier.",
            when 1440 =>
               "A parenthesized list brings named declarations into scop"
               & "e directly. There is no wildcard."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    import net/http (get, post)"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "This binds `get` and `post` and not the `http` namespace"
               & ". Every selected name must exist and be public, even if "
               & "the file never uses it. Each keeps its original declarat"
               & "ion's identity, type and mutability. The list is not emp"
               & "ty and has no trailing comma or renaming, and a selectio"
               & "n cannot be combined with an alias.",
            when 1450 =>
               "Each file has its own imports, so every file reads on it"
               & "s own."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Imports come first, before every declaration. One file's"
               & " imports are not visible in its siblings and are never r"
               & "e-exported. Binding one name twice in a file's imports i"
               & "s an error, whether it is the same import twice or a sel"
               & "ection colliding with an alias."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Shadowing goes like this:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- A parameter or local may shadow an import."
               & LF
               & "- A namespace import shadows a same-named module declara"
               & "tion only for qualified lookup; the module's unqualified"
               & " value stays visible."
               & LF
               & "- A selected name shadows the module declaration in both"
               & "."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Selecting a private member is reported differently from "
               & "selecting one that does not exist, and the report points"
               & " at the private declaration.",
            when 1460 =>
               "A value at module level must be known when the compiler "
               & "reads it, because nothing runs before the entry point. S"
               & "o it cannot call a function. Immutable values can stay i"
               & "n flash; mutable ones cost RAM, and the `mut` makes them"
               & " conspicuous."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    table: [4]u32 = [1, 2, 4, 8]"
               & LF
               & "    mut call_count: u32 = 0"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Integer arithmetic here is worked out in a wide range fi"
               & "rst, and only the final value is checked against its typ"
               & "e. So a module `value: u8 = 200 + 100 - 100` holds 200, "
               & "while the same `u8` addition inside a function overflows"
               & " and traps before the subtraction."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A value another module exports is known in the same way,"
               & " named through that module: `limit: u32 = sizes.page * 4"
               & "`, `ready: flags.state = flags.idle`. Qualifying a name "
               & "changes where it is found, not whether it is known."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A module struct may hold a function. The field names a d"
               & "eclared function, or an anonymous one that captures noth"
               & "ing, and what is stored is the routine's address; nothin"
               & "g is called. There is no all-zero function address [0540"
               & "], so such a struct must be written out in full."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Exact rules: spec [1940].",
            when 1470 =>
               "A package is a named collection of modules with a versio"
               & "n and an origin. Its name has two levels, owner and pack"
               & "age, and a directory under a search root is the package "
               & "it names."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A program holds one version of each package name. Two co"
               & "pies of a package would cost space a 32 KB part does not"
               & " have, and they would be different types, since types ar"
               & "e nominal. The whole program also keeps a single list of"
               & " which types conform to which concepts ([1240]), and two"
               & " copies would put conflicting entries in it. The compani"
               & "on tool, once it exists, will solve versions and report "
               & "a conflict as a hard error that asks for an upgrade."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The current compiler reads no versions or origins. It ta"
               & "kes the first matching module directory from the ordered"
               & " roots [1420], even when a later root holds another vers"
               & "ion.",
            when 1480 =>
               "You tell the compiler where modules are. `refine` takes "
               & "repeated `--root=DIR` options and then the entry module'"
               & "s directory, or its source files, which then form one mo"
               & "dule. With no `--root`, the named files form one synthet"
               & "ic module. The roots are searched in the order you give "
               & "([1420]), and that order alone decides: the compiler con"
               & "sults no home directory and adds no system root of its o"
               & "wn choosing."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "It does add one root: the library it ships with, found b"
               & "eside its executable. `core`, `hosted` and `platform` al"
               & "ways come from there, after every root you name. Because"
               & " the search is otherwise project-first, a project can ov"
               & "erride an ordinary module by putting its own copy in its"
               & " first root, and vendoring a package is just that."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Fetching packages, solving versions, lock files and nami"
               & "ng authority belong to a companion tool that will ship a"
               & "longside the compiler but stay separate from it: a compi"
               & "ler you can read is worth more than one that can downloa"
               & "d things. Arranging the roots so that only one version o"
               & "f each package is reachable is that tool's job, so until"
               & " it exists the compiler does not enforce [1470]'s one-ve"
               & "rsion rule. The intended root order is the project, then"
               & " the user's Landin home, then a system-wide one; whether"
               & " a path's leading segments name an owner and a package i"
               & "s a convention for people and that tool, while the compi"
               & "ler only sees directories."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Four source namespaces belong to the repository:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "| namespace | holds | available |"
               & LF
               & "|---|---|---|"
               & LF
               & "| `core` | the shared freestanding library: `core/mem`, "
               & "`core/text`, `core/vec` | every module and interface on "
               & "every target |"
               & LF
               & "| `hosted` | hosted providers such as `hosted/heap`, `ho"
               & "sted/system` and `hosted/blocking` | on hosted systems |"
               & LF
               & "| `platform` | scoped freestanding interfaces | `platfor"
               & "m/c` with an enabled LP64 C ABI; `platform/cortex_m` on "
               & "M-profile targets, higher feature levels included |"
               & LF
               & "| `landin` | the toolchain modules of [1560]: `landin/co"
               & "mpiler`, `landin/assembler`, `landin/linker` | everywher"
               & "e, without an import |"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Fine print:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- An import outside its target's scope is refused by nam"
               & "e when the target is checked, for example `hosted/heap` "
               & "in firmware."
               & LF
               & "- The toolchain modules are always there, so the bare na"
               & "mes `compiler`, `assembler` and `linker` cannot be decla"
               & "red or bound by an import, and an explicit `import landi"
               & "n/compiler` (or `assembler`, or `linker`) is refused by "
               & "name before any root is searched."
               & LF
               & "- Otherwise the namespace names are ordinary identifiers"
               & ".",
            when 430 =>
               "`ptr u32` is a pointer to a `u32`. `addr` takes an addre"
               & "ss, and `.val` is the thing pointed at, on either side o"
               & "f an assignment."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    mut value: u32 = 42"
               & LF
               & "    p: ptr mut u32 = addr value"
               & LF
               & "    v := p.val -- read through p"
               & LF
               & "    p.val = 43 -- write through p"
               & LF
               & "    ro: ptr u32 = addr value -- same address, read-only"
               & LF
               & "    -- ro.val = 1 -- error: ro cannot write"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "`mut` inside the type is what lets you write through the"
               & " pointer. `addr` of a mutable variable gives a `ptr mut`"
               & ", and `addr` of an immutable one a plain `ptr`."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "`addr p.val` is the address of the thing `p` points at, "
               & "and `addr items[i]` the address of a slice's element. `a"
               & "ddr p` is something else: the address of the variable `p"
               & "` itself, which may live on the stack.",
            when 440 =>
               "    add_up: (xs: []i32) -> (total: i32) ="
               & LF
               & "        total = 0"
               & LF
               & "        for x in xs do"
               & LF
               & "            total += x"
               & LF
               & "        end for"
               & LF
               & "    end add_up"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    add_written: (writable_slice: []mut i32) -> (total: "
               & "i32) ="
               & LF
               & "        total = add_up(writable_slice) -- fine: []mut i3"
               & "2 relaxes to []i32"
               & LF
               & "    end add_written"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "No bit changes; the callee simply forgets that it could "
               & "write. This is the only implicit relaxation in the langu"
               & "age, and it only goes this way: a read-only reference ne"
               & "ver becomes a writable one.",
            when 450 =>
               "Read-only applies to one level only. An immutable bindin"
               & "g, or an `in` parameter, protects the value it holds, no"
               & "t what that value points at. A list handed over as `in` "
               & "still has writable elements if its storage is writable."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Protecting deeper would mean knowing which references a "
               & "value owns and which it borrows, and Landin does not tra"
               & "ck ownership ([0910]). When you want to hand out a read-"
               & "only view, hand out a read-only reference; an accessor i"
               & "s how.",
            when 460 =>
               "`ptr(...)` turns an address into a pointer. The pointer "
               & "type comes from the declaration."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    gpio: ptr mut u32 = ptr(0x4001_0000)"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "This binding is immutable, but the register behind it is"
               & " written: the two questions of [0070] really are separat"
               & "e. Nothing in the pointer's type makes access to it a de"
               & "vice transaction. The operation that reads or writes doe"
               & "s that ([0850]).",
            when 470 =>
               "A pointer and an integer convert both ways like any othe"
               & "r conversion ([0700]): apply the type. Here `gpio` is th"
               & "e pointer from [0460]."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    gpio_round_trip: () -> (back: ptr mut u32) ="
               & LF
               & "        address := usize(gpio) -- pointer to integer"
               & LF
               & "        back = ptr(address) -- integer to pointer: no or"
               & "igin"
               & LF
               & "    end gpio_round_trip"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The cost is on the way back. An integer has no origin, s"
               & "o a pointer made from one borrows nothing as far as the "
               & "compiler knows, and it will say nothing more about that "
               & "pointer's lifetime. This is the one place inside the lan"
               & "guage where lifetime checking is left behind, much as at"
               & " the C boundary.",
            when 480 =>
               """Maybe a pointer"" is a union of an atom and a pointer ty"
               & "pe. With a single atom, the compiler stores it as a plai"
               & "n pointer, with 0 meaning the atom."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    none_found: atom"
               & LF
               & "    maybe_ptr: type = none_found | ptr mut u32"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    lookup: (key: u32, cell: ptr mut u32) -> (found: may"
               & "be_ptr from cell) ="
               & LF
               & "        if key == 0 then"
               & LF
               & "            found = none_found"
               & LF
               & "        else"
               & LF
               & "            found = cell"
               & LF
               & "        end if"
               & LF
               & "    end lookup"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    read: (m: maybe_ptr, fallback: u32) -> (value: u32) "
               & "="
               & LF
               & "        match m"
               & LF
               & "            none_found: value = fallback"
               & LF
               & "            ptr (p): value = p.val"
               & LF
               & "        end match"
               & LF
               & "    end read"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A pointer goes into the union with no ceremony. Getting "
               & "it out takes a `match`, and the `match` has to name both"
               & " cases: `ptr (p)` is the present case, with the pointer "
               & "bound to `p`."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "To ask only which case it holds, compare with the atom: "
               & "`m == none_found` or `m <> none_found`. That reads no ad"
               & "dress, so it needs no `match`. Two such unions do not co"
               & "mpare with each other."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A union may also hold several atoms beside the pointer; "
               & "it then takes two cells instead of one."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Exact rules: spec [1870].",
            when 490 =>
               "Before you reach for a pointer, see whether one of these"
               & " does the job:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "| you want | use |"
               & LF
               & "|---|---|"
               & LF
               & "| a run of elements, to read or to write | a slice ([057"
               & "0]) |"
               & LF
               & "| one element of an array you already have | its index |"
               & LF
               & "| a service such as an allocator | a handle: a small val"
               & "ue that refers to state kept elsewhere ([1360]) |"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Each says more than a pointer would: a slice carries its"
               & " length, an index is checked against that length, and a "
               & "handle's type says what it is for.",
            when 500 =>
               "There is no `p + n` on pointers, and no built-in operati"
               & "on that turns a pointer into a slice. Code that really n"
               & "eeds to compute an address converts to an integer and ba"
               & "ck ([0470]), and gives up lifetime checking when it does"
               & ". Inside the standard library, `core/mem` does this for "
               & "you, behind its private storage operations. If you need "
               & "an element's address, take it from a slice that you know"
               & " is not empty: `addr items[0]`.",
            when 510 =>
               "A growing array has room for more items than it holds. T"
               & "he empty slots contain no `t` yet, so no slice may cover"
               & " them: a `[]mut t` over them would claim values that do "
               & "not exist. Neither may they be zero-filled, because many"
               & " types, pointers among them, have no zero value ([0540])"
               & "."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "`core/mem`'s `storage(t)` is the answer. It holds a bloc"
               & "k of storage, counts how many slots at its start are ini"
               & "tialized, and only ever shows you that initialized prefi"
               & "x. Its representation is the private type `raw(t)`: you "
               & "can hold one but not look inside."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "| operation | what it does |"
               & LF
               & "|---|---|"
               & LF
               & "| `capacity`, `length` | how many slots there are, and h"
               & "ow many are initialized |"
               & LF
               & "| `admit` | initialize the next slot |"
               & LF
               & "| `get`, `replace` | read or overwrite an initialized sl"
               & "ot |"
               & LF
               & "| `used` | the initialized prefix as a `[]mut t` |"
               & LF
               & "| `withdraw`, `clear` | drop the last item, or every ite"
               & "m |"
               & LF
               & "| `transfer` | copy one item onto the end of another `st"
               & "orage`, for growing |"
               & LF
               & "| `dispose` | hand the block back once it is empty |"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The four ways of asking for something impossible are ord"
               & "inary failures: `full`, `out_of_bounds`, `empty` and `no"
               & "t_empty`."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "To grow, reserve a bigger block and `transfer` every ite"
               & "m into it. A `transfer` copies and leaves its source as "
               & "it was, so the old block is unchanged until you are done"
               & ": if a step fails, release the new block and carry on wi"
               & "th the old one. Only after every item is copied do you e"
               & "mpty and dispose of the old block and publish the new on"
               & "e."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "This keeps the empty slots out of reach. It does not mak"
               & "e raw storage safe: the allocation you supply must be li"
               & "ve, aligned and as large as you say ([1720]).",
            when 770 =>
               "A pointer to a local dies with the routine. Returning on"
               & "e, or storing it anywhere that outlives the routine, is "
               & "an error."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    bad: () -> (p: ptr u32) ="
               & LF
               & "        x: u32 = 42"
               & LF
               & "        p = addr x -- error: a frame origin escapes"
               & LF
               & "    end bad"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A pointer to module storage is static, so the same shape"
               & " is fine:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    mut tally: u32 = 0"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    tally_cell: () -> (p: ptr mut u32) ="
               & LF
               & "        p = addr tally -- fine: module storage is static"
               & LF
               & "    end tally_cell"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The origin travels with the value: a slice of a local ar"
               & "ray, or an `any` built over a local ([1370]), is frame t"
               & "oo.",
            when 780 =>
               "A parameter is non-escaping by default. The callee may r"
               & "ead and write through a pointer it was handed, but it ma"
               & "y not keep it once it returns."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    increment_cell: (cell: ptr mut u32) -> none ="
               & LF
               & "        cell.val += 1 -- using the pointer is fine; keep"
               & "ing it would not be"
               & LF
               & "    end increment_cell"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    caller_of_increment: () -> (n: u32) ="
               & LF
               & "        mut local: u32 = 1"
               & LF
               & "        increment_cell(addr local) -- fine: the callee d"
               & "oes not keep it"
               & LF
               & "        n = local"
               & LF
               & "    end caller_of_increment"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "So a pointer to a local can go into any call whose param"
               & "eter does not say `escaping`. When a routine does keep a"
               & " reference, it marks the parameter `escaping`, and then "
               & "the caller may only pass something that lives long enoug"
               & "h: allocated or static, never frame."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Assume a struct `node` with a field `next: ptr mut node`"
               & ". `mem.new` allocates one value through the allocator it"
               & " is given and returns a pointer to it ([1360]; `core/mem"
               & "` in the library guide)."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    push_front: (escaping inout head: ptr mut node, esca"
               & "ping item: ptr mut node)"
               & LF
               & "                -> none ="
               & LF
               & "        item.val.next = head"
               & LF
               & "        head = item"
               & LF
               & "    end push_front"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    build: (provider: type is mem.allocator, memory: pro"
               & "vider,"
               & LF
               & "            escaping seed: ptr mut node)"
               & LF
               & "           -> (head: ptr mut node) ! mem.out_of_memory ="
               & LF
               & "        head = seed"
               & LF
               & "        n := try mem.new(memory: memory, value: seed.val"
               & ")"
               & LF
               & "        push_front(head, n) -- allocated, fine"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "        mut local: node = seed.val"
               & LF
               & "        push_front(head, addr local) -- error: a frame o"
               & "rigin escapes"
               & LF
               & "    end build"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "`push_front` stores each argument where the other can re"
               & "ach it, so both are `escaping`."
               & LF
               & LF
               & """Keeping"" means any store that outlives the call: into a"
               & " module binding, or through a pointer, slice or `inout` "
               & "field the caller owns. Storing a reference back into sto"
               & "rage of the same origin is an update, not keeping, and n"
               & "eeds no marker. Storing one parameter's reference into a"
               & "nother parameter's storage needs `escaping`."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Or it needs `escaping into`, which names the parameter w"
               & "hose storage keeps it, and no other. Then a frame node c"
               & "an go onto a frame list, as long as it lives as long as "
               & "the list: it is a parameter of the caller, static, alloc"
               & "ated, or a local declared before the list. Here each par"
               & "ameter is kept in the other's storage, so each names the"
               & " other."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    push_kept: (escaping into item inout head: ptr mut n"
               & "ode,"
               & LF
               & "                escaping into head item: ptr mut node) -"
               & "> none ="
               & LF
               & "        item.val.next = head"
               & LF
               & "        head = item"
               & LF
               & "    end push_kept"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    on_the_frame: (escaping seed: ptr mut node) -> none "
               & "="
               & LF
               & "        mut first: node = seed.val"
               & LF
               & "        mut head: ptr mut node = seed"
               & LF
               & "        push_kept(head, addr first) -- fine: first is de"
               & "clared before head"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "        mut late: node = seed.val"
               & LF
               & "        push_kept(head, addr late) -- error: late is dec"
               & "lared after head"
               & LF
               & "    end on_the_frame"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The routine may keep `item` in `head` and nowhere else. "
               & "A frame reference never goes into a parameter's storage,"
               & " and a reference from the caller's parameter goes into a"
               & "nything its frame holds."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Exact rules: spec [1910].",
            when 830 =>
               "A view taken from a local borrows it. While the view is "
               & "still in use, the local cannot be passed as `inout` or `"
               & "sink`, because that call could grow, move or replace the"
               & " storage under the view."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    reset_cells: (inout buffer: [4]i32) -> none ="
               & LF
               & "        buffer = [9, 9, 9, 9]"
               & LF
               & "    end reset_cells"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    view_then_reset: () -> (n: i32) ="
               & LF
               & "        mut buffer: [4]i32 = [1, 2, 3, 4]"
               & LF
               & "        xs := buffer[0..<2]"
               & LF
               & "        reset_cells(buffer) -- error: buffer is borrowed"
               & " by xs"
               & LF
               & "        n = xs[0]"
               & LF
               & "    end view_then_reset"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The fix is always nearby: finish with the view before th"
               & "e call, or take it again after. The check looks at one r"
               & "outine body, so there is nothing to annotate."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Only `inout` and `sink` trigger it, since they are the t"
               & "wo ways a call can change the storage itself. Reading th"
               & "e local, or passing it `in` or by pointer, is fine while"
               & " the view lives. An unchecked region ([1120]) does not t"
               & "urn the check off: origins and escape are among the chec"
               & "ks that stay.",
            when 790 =>
               "When a routine returns a reference derived from a parame"
               & "ter, its signature says so with `from`. The caller then "
               & "knows that the thing it passed in has to hold still whil"
               & "e it uses the result."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Here a `batch` holds a writable slice of readings and ho"
               & "w many of them are filled in:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    batch: type = struct"
               & LF
               & "        items: []mut u32"
               & LF
               & "        count: usize"
               & LF
               & "    end batch"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    filled_part: (b: batch) -> (s: []mut u32 from b) = b"
               & ".items[0..<b.count] end"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- `from` names where the reference came from; it says no"
               & "thing about writing. Write permission is the return type"
               & "'s business ([0430])."
               & LF
               & "- `from a, b` borrows from both."
               & LF
               & "- No `from` at all means the result is independent: it b"
               & "orrows nothing. That is what an allocator returns, which"
               & " is why two live allocations from one allocator are unre"
               & "markable."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "One accessor is enough. `filled_part` hands out the wide"
               & "st permission the storage has, and a caller who wants le"
               & "ss relaxes it ([0440]): `xs: []u32 = filled_part(b)`. Th"
               & "ere is no const and non-const pair."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The clause is checked against the body in both direction"
               & "s. Returning something derived from a parameter without "
               & "naming it is an error, and so is naming a parameter the "
               & "result did not come from."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "`from` is the mirror of `escaping`. `escaping` says the "
               & "callee keeps what the caller handed in; `from` says the "
               & "caller keeps something of what the callee handed back. B"
               & "oth are written, not inferred, so a distant body can nev"
               & "er change a signature, and function pointers, concept en"
               & "tries and the C boundary need no special rule."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Two refinements: a read-only result that points into sta"
               & "tic storage may name a parameter it does not come from, "
               & "since static storage outlives every parameter; and a wri"
               & "table result must name every place it could point to, so"
               & " a helper that might return module storage takes it as a"
               & " parameter and names that too."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Exact rules: spec [1910].",
            when 800 =>
               "A result with `from` borrows its source just as a view d"
               & "oes in [0830]. The signature supplies the derivation ins"
               & "tead of one expression showing it, and the fix is the sa"
               & "me: take the view again afterwards."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "`core/vec`'s `vec.used` is declared with `from`, like [0"
               & "790]'s `filled_part`:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    import core/mem"
               & LF
               & "    import core/vec"
               & LF
               & "    import hosted/heap"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    grow_under_view: (inout numbers: vec.list(i32)) -> ("
               & "n: i32) ! mem.out_of_memory ="
               & LF
               & "        memory := heap.new()"
               & LF
               & "        xs := vec.used(numbers)"
               & LF
               & "        try vec.push(numbers, memory, 5) -- error: numbe"
               & "rs is borrowed by xs"
               & LF
               & "        n = xs[0]"
               & LF
               & "    end grow_under_view"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Nothing is written at the call. The convention lives in "
               & "the declaration, and a caller that misuses the result is"
               & " told at its next use, just as with `escaping`.",
            when 840 =>
               "When a value combines origins, it takes the most restric"
               & "tive one. A struct holding one frame pointer is frame as"
               & " a whole, and a value chosen between two branches takes "
               & "the shorter-lived of the two."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A value that holds no references has no origin and goes "
               & "anywhere, which is one more reason the idiom is handles "
               & "and indices. A number computed from a view keeps no borr"
               & "ow: saving `lenof xs` saves a number, usable after `xs` "
               & "or its container changes."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Taking an address or a range is different: that referenc"
               & "e keeps the selected storage's origin ([0430], [0790]). "
               & "Writing through a known address of a local updates the l"
               & "ocal's origins too. Mixing in a pointer made from an int"
               & "eger ([0810]) does not hide a tracked origin."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Exact rules: spec [1910].",
            when 810 =>
               "Converting an integer to a pointer ([0470]) ends trackin"
               & "g. The integer has no origin, so the pointer borrows not"
               & "hing, and the compiler says nothing more about its lifet"
               & "ime. It also does not check that the storage is live or "
               & "large enough: that stays the library's unsafe obligation"
               & " ([1720])."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    block_at: (base: ptr mut u8, offset: usize) -> (bloc"
               & "k: ptr mut u8) ="
               & LF
               & "        address: usize = usize(base) + offset"
               & LF
               & "        block = ptr(address) -- no tracked derivation su"
               & "rvives"
               & LF
               & "    end block_at"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "This is how `core/arena` returns a block without borrowi"
               & "ng the arena, after checking its arithmetic; `core` gets"
               & " no special privilege for it. [0500] says why there is n"
               & "o `offset` or `slice_from` to do this for you, and [0510"
               & "] keeps uninitialized storage out of reach."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Tracking comes back through a signature. A routine that "
               & "indexes a table C gave it writes `from table` on its res"
               & "ult, and callers are checked against that like any other"
               & " signature. Its own body is not checked, since an intege"
               & "r-made pointer satisfies any `from`, so the contract is "
               & "its author's claim: keep such routines small.",
            when 820 =>
               "An arena is a library value from `core/arena`: you hand "
               & "it a block of bytes, it hands out pieces of it, and indi"
               & "vidual frees do nothing. It is not a builtin type or blo"
               & "ck. Code that takes any allocator accepts it as a `mem.a"
               & "llocator` provider."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    import core/arena"
               & LF
               & "    import core/mem"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    mut backing: [1024]u8 = zeroed"
               & LF
               & "    report: () -> (code: i32) ="
               & LF
               & "        mut space: arena.space = zeroed"
               & LF
               & "        scratch := arena.over(space, addr backing[0], 10"
               & "24)"
               & LF
               & "        code = 1"
               & LF
               & "        first := mem.new(scratch, u8(7)) else (problem)"
               & LF
               & "            _ = problem"
               & LF
               & "            return"
               & LF
               & "        end"
               & LF
               & "        second := mem.new(scratch, u8(9)) else (problem)"
               & LF
               & "            _ = problem"
               & LF
               & "            return"
               & LF
               & "        end"
               & LF
               & "        code = i32(first.val) + i32(second.val)"
               & LF
               & "    end report"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- `arena.over(state, base, size)` takes the backing addr"
               & "ess, its size in bytes, and an `arena.space` you declare"
               & " `zeroed` beside it. It returns the arena's handle `from"
               & "` the space and the backing; copies of the handle share "
               & "one arena."
               & LF
               & "- Allocation aligns the address and advances an offset. "
               & "A request that does not fit fails with `mem.out_of_memor"
               & "y` before changing anything."
               & LF
               & "- `arena.reset` gives everything back at once, and `aren"
               & "a.used` reports the bytes consumed."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "`over` takes its backing as `escaping base`, so it accep"
               & "ts module storage or storage from another allocator and "
               & "refuses a frame array. Allocations borrow nothing ([0790"
               & "]), so a helper could store one in module storage, and a"
               & "n arena over frame storage would let that pointer outliv"
               & "e the frame. For a local buffer whose every use stays in"
               & "side the frame, `arena.over_unchecked` takes frame backi"
               & "ng; you must stop using its results before the buffer en"
               & "ds."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Other providers build on the same idea:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "| provider | what it does | what you must do |"
               & LF
               & "|---|---|---|"
               & LF
               & "| `core/region.over(ledger, provider)` | records every e"
               & "xtent it takes from `provider` in a `region.space` ledge"
               & "r you declare `zeroed` | release all at once with `defer"
               & " region.release(ledger, provider)`, and stop using its a"
               & "llocations before then |"
               & LF
               & "| `core/pool.over` | hands out pieces of backing whose o"
               & "rigin its handle keeps | keep the backing live until eve"
               & "ry result is done with |"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A region's handle holds a copy of its provider's handle."
               & " Its allocations borrow nothing, its bookkeeping also us"
               & "es the provider's capacity, and a finite provider stays "
               & "finite: there is no hidden heap fallback."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "What the compiler does not do for you: arrange the backi"
               & "ng's lifetime and release (`defer`, [1100], runs on ever"
               & "y exit), keep overlapping backing apart, or stop you usi"
               & "ng a result after its backing ends or is reused. Results"
               & " obey the ordinary origin rules and nothing more. `arena"
               & "` itself is not a keyword: it is an ordinary name you ma"
               & "y declare.",
            when 850 =>
               "A volatile access is exempt from the borrow rule and fro"
               & "m every aliasing assumption. It is an operation, not a p"
               & "ointer type:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "| operation | for |"
               & LF
               & "|---|---|"
               & LF
               & "| `compiler.volatile_load`, `compiler.volatile_store` | "
               & "one scalar |"
               & LF
               & "| `compiler.register_read`, `compiler.register_write` | "
               & "a register image ([0740]) |"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Each performs exactly one access of its width through an"
               & " ordinary pointer, usually one built from an address ([0"
               & "460]) and so already outside the lifetime checks ([0470]"
               & "). Hardware often needs several typed windows on one add"
               & "ress, a byte view and a word view of the same register, "
               & "and that is legal on purpose. There is no `volatile ptr`"
               & ": a second permission on every reference would buy nothi"
               & "ng the operations do not."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "DMA uses ordinary slices. Landin makes no lifetime promi"
               & "se about storage a device is still writing: neither an i"
               & "nterrupt nor masking one makes it reusable, and waiting "
               & "for the device is the driver's own protocol."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Exact rules: spec [1990].",
            when 860 =>
               "The checks know which kind of storage a reference points"
               & " into, not which allocation or whether it still exists. "
               & "So these go through:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- Use after free or reset. The compiler does not know wh"
               & "ich arena or allocator a pointer came from, or that it h"
               & "as been given back."
               & LF
               & "- A pointer that went through longer-lived storage. Insi"
               & "de one routine a struct carries its fields' origins ([08"
               & "40]). Store a pointer into a heap node, though, and read"
               & " it back later through the node: it now has the node's o"
               & "rigin, and whatever it pointed to may be gone."
               & LF
               & "- Anything across the C boundary."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Catching these would need regions or generation counters"
               & ", and Landin deliberately has neither.",
            when 1230 =>
               "A concept names a bundle of operations, each a complete "
               & "function signature written in terms of the concept's typ"
               & "e parameter."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    ordered: type = concept (t: type)"
               & LF
               & "        less: (a: t, b: t) -> (yes: bool)"
               & LF
               & "    end ordered"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A concept is an ordinary type declaration, so it lives i"
               & "n a module like any other name. It has no default bodies"
               & ": every entry is supplied by each conformance.",
            when 1240 =>
               "A conformance registers one type against one concept, na"
               & "ming the function that supplies each entry."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    less_than_i32: (a: i32, b: i32) -> (yes: bool) = a <"
               & " b end less_than_i32"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    i32 is ordered (less: less_than_i32)"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Each label is an entry of the concept, and each supplyin"
               & "g function must have exactly the entry's signature with "
               & "`t` replaced by `i32`. It must be a declared function: ["
               & "1010]'s `less_i32`, a binding that holds an anonymous fu"
               & "nction, cannot supply an entry. There is no implicit con"
               & "formance: a type with a matching `less` is still not `or"
               & "dered` until a line like this one says so.",
            when 1290 =>
               "A generic function takes the type as an ordinary paramet"
               & "er, constrained with `is`, and calls the concept's entri"
               & "es through that parameter."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    sort: (t: type is ordered, data: []mut t) -> none ="
               & LF
               & "        for k in 1..<lenof data do"
               & LF
               & "            mut j := k"
               & LF
               & "            while j > 0 and t.less(data[j], data[j - 1])"
               & " do"
               & LF
               & "                tmp := data[j]"
               & LF
               & "                data[j] = data[j - 1]"
               & LF
               & "                data[j - 1] = tmp"
               & LF
               & "                dec j"
               & LF
               & "            end while"
               & LF
               & "        end for"
               & LF
               & "    end sort"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "`t.less` is the `less` of whatever type `t` turns out to"
               & " be. Because entries are reached through the type parame"
               & "ter, two constrained parameters never collide: `left_typ"
               & "e.less` and `right_type.less` are different functions."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Type and `fixed` parameters exist only at compile time; "
               & "they add no runtime argument. A `fixed` parameter is an "
               & "integer known at compile time, and inside the body it ma"
               & "y also be used as an ordinary value. The order of parame"
               & "ters does not matter, so `fill: (inout data: [capacity]u"
               & "8, fixed capacity: u32)` is as good as putting `capacity"
               & "` first."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A type parameter may also be unconstrained, for code tha"
               & "t only moves values around. Fine print:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- Every type parameter is written in the list. A name th"
               & "at is not in scope is a misspelling and an error, never "
               & "a new type variable, just as a misspelt build option is "
               & "an error ([1530])."
               & LF
               & "- A generic function is not a function value. Only a cal"
               & "l picks a concrete instance, so you cannot take `sort` i"
               & "tself and store it."
               & LF
               & "- A private `! ...` error set is inferred separately for"
               & " each concrete instance.",
            when 1300 =>
               "The type is normally deduced from the arguments. You may"
               & " also name it, in the same named-argument list as the ru"
               & "ntime arguments."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    sort_demo: (values: []mut i32) -> none ="
               & LF
               & "        sort(values)"
               & LF
               & "        sort(t: i32, data: values)"
               & LF
               & "    end sort_demo"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "It is all or nothing: either every type and `fixed` para"
               & "meter is deduced, or the call names every one of them. R"
               & "untime arguments keep [0980]'s rule, positional first an"
               & "d then named."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Deduction matches each written parameter type against th"
               & "e argument's type, exactly. It never uses the return typ"
               & "e, a conversion or a search of the conformances, and it "
               & "runs no code. Two consequences:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- A writable `[]mut i32` argument still passes where `[]"
               & "t` is written ([0440]), but deeper inside a type, permis"
               & "sion must match exactly."
               & LF
               & "- A fixed bound written `capacity * 2` is never solved f"
               & "or `capacity`; it is only checked once another argument "
               & "has fixed `capacity`."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Exact rules: spec [1920].",
            when 1310 =>
               "A call to a generic function passes, invisibly, a table "
               & "for each conformance the body uses: the type's size and "
               & "alignment, then the concept's functions in the order the"
               & " concept declares them. `t.less(...)` loads `less` from "
               & "that table and calls it. The same tables carry `any C` v"
               & "alues ([1370]), so static generics and runtime dispatch "
               & "are one mechanism ([1690])."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The tables are the meaning of generic code. Specializati"
               & "on, replacing an indirect call with a direct one where t"
               & "he compiler can prove which table arrives, is an optimiz"
               & "ation that never changes what a program does. Two indepe"
               & "ndent switches control code generation:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "| option | values | default |"
               & LF
               & "|---|---|---|"
               & LF
               & "| `--optimize` | `none`, `size`, `speed` | `size` |"
               & LF
               & "| `--specialize` | `off`, `auto`, `all` | `auto` |"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Neither depends on `--build-mode`. A call is never speci"
               & "alized merely because its source type is known: the comp"
               & "iler needs proof of the table that actually arrives. Cal"
               & "ls through `any`, through a table it cannot trace, or in"
               & " an instance whose address is taken stay indirect. `--bu"
               & "ild-report=PATH` writes a report of what was specialized"
               & " and what stayed indirect, and why.",
            when 1340 =>
               "A concept may build on others by naming them after `is`."
               & " A type that satisfies the combined concept still confor"
               & "ms to each part separately."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Assume a struct `button` with a `text` field, a type `ca"
               & "nvas`, and functions `button_draw`, `button_click` and `"
               & "button_focus` with the entries' signatures."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    drawable: type = concept (t: type)"
               & LF
               & "        draw: (self: ptr t, target: ptr mut canvas) -> n"
               & "one"
               & LF
               & "    end drawable"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    clickable: type = concept (t: type)"
               & LF
               & "        click: (self: ptr mut t, x: u32, y: u32) -> none"
               & LF
               & "    end clickable"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    widget: type = concept (t: type) is drawable, clicka"
               & "ble"
               & LF
               & "        focus: (self: ptr mut t) -> none"
               & LF
               & "    end widget"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    button is drawable (draw: button_draw)"
               & LF
               & "    button is clickable (click: button_click)"
               & LF
               & "    button is widget (focus: button_focus)"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "All three conformances are needed, because `widget`'s su"
               & "pplies only what `widget` adds. Having the parts does no"
               & "t make a type a `widget`, and being a `widget` does not "
               & "make it `drawable`; conformance is always declared, even"
               & " when its list is empty."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The combined concept has a name of its own, so an `any w"
               & "idget` value still carries one table and stays two words"
               & "."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "In generic code, `t.entry` must name exactly one declara"
               & "tion across the concept and all its parents. A parent re"
               & "ached twice, through two paths, is still one concept, bu"
               & "t two different concepts that both declare `entry` make "
               & "`t.entry` ambiguous. Parent order never decides, and a c"
               & "hild's own entry does not override an inherited one. A c"
               & "lash nobody uses is not an error."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Exact rules: spec [1920].",
            when 1350 =>
               "A type takes parameters through its own declaration form"
               & ". It is not a function that returns a type: that would b"
               & "e compile-time execution, which Landin does not have. Su"
               & "bstitution, not execution."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    pair: type (t: type) = struct"
               & LF
               & "        first: t"
               & LF
               & "        second: t"
               & LF
               & "    end pair"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "`pair(i32)` and `pair(u8)` are different types, and ever"
               & "y use of `pair(i32)` is the same type. A growable contai"
               & "ner is generic the same way: `core/vec`'s `list(t)` is o"
               & "ne, and [0510] explains how it keeps its empty slots out"
               & " of sight. A type parameter may carry one concept constr"
               & "aint, and a `fixed` parameter is an integer known at com"
               & "pile time. Here `cmp.hashable` is a concept `core/cmp` p"
               & "rovides, and `zeroable` is [0550]'s:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    import core/cmp"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    key_value: type (key_type: type is cmp.hashable, val"
               & "ue_type: type) = struct"
               & LF
               & "        key: key_type"
               & LF
               & "        value: value_type"
               & LF
               & "    end key_value"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    cleared: type (t: type is zeroable, fixed capacity: "
               & "u32) = struct"
               & LF
               & "        items: [capacity]t"
               & LF
               & "        len: u32"
               & LF
               & "    end cleared"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Applications are positional and complete: `key_value(u32"
               & ", utf8)`, never `key_value(u32)`. A `fixed` argument is "
               & "an integer literal, or a `fixed` parameter passed on by "
               & "another generic declaration, and it must fit its declare"
               & "d type. Whether an argument type may go where the body p"
               & "uts it, as a field or an array element, is checked after"
               & " substitution."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "An alias may be generic too, including a union of atom s"
               & "ets, and an alias never makes a new type: `missing | ptr"
               & " t` keeps the one-word optional pointer of [0480]. Fine "
               & "print:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- The template is checked even if nothing applies it, an"
               & "d an error that depends on the argument is reported at e"
               & "ach application."
               & LF
               & "- An unused or phantom parameter still distinguishes ins"
               & "tances."
               & LF
               & "- A struct may not contain itself by value, even through"
               & " another template.",
            when 1250 =>
               "A conformance for a generic type declares its type varia"
               & "bles in front, because it holds for every one of them."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The examples use two concepts met later: `iterable`, whi"
               & "ch `for` loops use ([1320]), and `mem.allocator` ([1360]"
               & "). `counted(provider)` is an allocator that wraps anothe"
               & "r, and the `pair_...` and `counted_...` names are the fu"
               & "nctions that supply each entry."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    (t: type) pair(t) is iterable (cur: u8, item_type: t"
               & ","
               & LF
               & "                                   first: pair_first, at"
               & "_end: pair_at_end,"
               & LF
               & "                                   item: pair_item, next"
               & ": pair_next)"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    (provider: type is mem.allocator) counted(provider) "
               & "is mem.allocator"
               & LF
               & "        (alloc: counted_alloc, grow: counted_grow, resiz"
               & "e: counted_resize,"
               & LF
               & "         free: counted_free)"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The binder is an ordinary parameter list, the same form "
               & "functions and generic types take, so a variable may carr"
               & "y a constraint and a `fixed` parameter may appear among "
               & "them. The supplying functions are generic themselves; in"
               & "stantiating the conformance gives them their type argume"
               & "nt and leaves functions of exactly the concept's shape."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The target must be the whole generic type applied to the"
               & " binder in order, each variable used once: one conforman"
               & "ce covers the whole family `pair(t)`. Another family for"
               & " the same template and concept, or a concrete exception "
               & "such as `pair(u8) is iterable`, is a collision ([1280])."
               & " Without this form no generic container could be travers"
               & "ed, sorted or handed to other generic code.",
            when 1270 =>
               "A conformance is keyed by the type, the concept and the "
               & "concept's other input types, so one type may satisfy the"
               & " same concept more than once."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Assume `position` is a byte-offset type, and `utf8_nth` "
               & "and `utf8_at_pos` look up a character by count and by po"
               & "sition."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    indexable: type = concept (t: type, idx: type, item_"
               & "type: type)"
               & LF
               & "        get: (s: t, i: idx) -> (item: item_type)"
               & LF
               & "    end indexable"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    utf8 is indexable (idx: usize, item_type: u32, get: "
               & "utf8_nth)"
               & LF
               & "    utf8 is indexable (idx: position, item_type: u32, ge"
               & "t: utf8_at_pos)"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The labels for the extra inputs, `idx` and `item_type`, "
               & "are part of the key; the function labels are not."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Which concepts exist, and how finely they distinguish wh"
               & "at an operation costs, is the standard library's busines"
               & "s. The language checks nothing about cost: a guarantee s"
               & "hould not depend on how clever the compiler happens to b"
               & "e.",
            when 1280 =>
               "A conformance may be declared in any module, and two for"
               & " the same key are an error. There is no precedence, no o"
               & "rphan rule and no override: the compiler reports the col"
               & "lision and somebody sorts it out."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "When two libraries collide, you do not have to change ei"
               & "ther. Wrap the type in a `distinct` type of your own and"
               & " conform that, or pass the functions explicitly instead "
               & "of through a concept."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Every conformance in every module the program reaches is"
               & " collected before any generic call is checked, whether o"
               & "r not anything uses it. A conformance is a registration,"
               & " not a name, so it needs no `public` and refuses one. Mo"
               & "dules the program does not reach contribute nothing.",
            when 1260 =>
               "A concept entry's error set is written out or absent, ne"
               & "ver inferred with `! ...`. An entry is reached through a"
               & " table, [0960] forbids an inferred set there, and so the"
               & " concept fixes the error set once for every type that wi"
               & "ll ever satisfy it."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "That is right for allocation, where out of memory is out"
               & " of memory, but it shapes how you design a concept. The "
               & "same goes for what an implementation is handed: by conve"
               & "ntion, a concept with no allocator among its parameters "
               & "says its implementations do not allocate. The compiler d"
               & "oes not check that, since any hosted routine can make a "
               & "heap of its own ([1680])."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Resist widening a concept until it fits its hungriest im"
               & "plementation; that hands every implementation the sum of"
               & " everyone's needs. Either the hungry one picks a shape t"
               & "hat does not need it, or there are two concepts.",
            when 1320 =>
               "`for x in s` over your own type uses the `iterable` conc"
               & "ept. Conform to it and your type works in a `for` loop."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    iterable: type = concept (t: type, cur: type, item_t"
               & "ype: type)"
               & LF
               & "        first: (s: ptr t) -> (c: cur)"
               & LF
               & "        at_end: (s: ptr t, c: cur) -> (yes: bool)"
               & LF
               & "        item: (s: ptr t, c: cur) -> (v: item_type)"
               & LF
               & "        next: (s: ptr t, c: cur) -> (c2: cur)"
               & LF
               & "    end iterable"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The conformance supplies the cursor type `cur` and the `"
               & "item_type`, as [1250]'s `pair` does. The loop evaluates "
               & "`s` once and keeps a private copy; each provider gets a "
               & "read-only pointer to that copy, so a large struct is cop"
               & "ied once, not on every call. The labels may come in any "
               & "order. The four signatures are exact: no permission, err"
               & "or set or `from` clause is inferred or converted, so a p"
               & "rovider whose result is declared `from s` does not match"
               & "."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "`item` returns a fresh value for the loop binding, never"
               & " an alias into the source. A container that wants to han"
               & "d out references into its storage returns a slice view i"
               & "nstead and uses the built-in slice traversal, as `for va"
               & "lue in vec.used(numbers)` does."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A source that can fail while streaming conforms to `fall"
               & "ible_iterable`, which adds an error set as a fourth inpu"
               & "t:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    fallible_iterable: type = concept"
               & LF
               & "        (t: type, cur: type, item_type: type, errors: ty"
               & "pe)"
               & LF
               & "        first: (s: ptr t) -> (c: cur) ! errors"
               & LF
               & "        at_end: (s: ptr t, c: cur) -> (yes: bool) ! erro"
               & "rs"
               & LF
               & "        item: (s: ptr t, c: cur) -> (v: item_type) ! err"
               & "ors"
               & LF
               & "        next: (s: ptr t, c: cur) -> (c2: cur) ! errors"
               & LF
               & "    end fallible_iterable"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "`errors` is an atom set, and all four entries declare ex"
               & "actly that set; a provider may declare an error it never"
               & " raises. Such a source is walked with `try for` ([0960])"
               & ". `try for` needs one unambiguous conformance and prefer"
               & "s `fallible_iterable` when both exist; plain `for` only "
               & "ever uses `iterable`.",
            when 1330 =>
               "In `for i in 0..<10 do`, the loop evaluates the two boun"
               & "ds once and counts between them directly ([1150]). `0..<"
               & "10` and `0..9` are loop and slice syntax ([0360]), not e"
               & "xpressions, and the header does not go through `iterable"
               & "`."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "For other stepping, write a struct with its own `iterabl"
               & "e` conformance. A library could then offer `for i in ste"
               & "p(0, 10, 2) do ... end for`; `core` has no such `step` o"
               & "r `step_range` today.",
            when 1360 =>
               "An allocator is anything that conforms to `allocator`. T"
               & "he same container runs on the heap, in an arena or on a "
               & "fixed buffer, and a deliberately failing allocator makes"
               & " the out-of-memory paths testable, which almost nobody b"
               & "others with in C."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "This is `core/mem`'s concept:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    allocator: type = concept (provider: type)"
               & LF
               & "        alloc: (self: provider, size: usize, alignment: "
               & "usize)"
               & LF
               & "               -> (p: ptr mut u8) ! out_of_memory"
               & LF
               & "        grow: (self: provider, p: ptr mut u8, old_size: "
               & "usize,"
               & LF
               & "               new_size: usize, alignment: usize) -> (gr"
               & "own: bool)"
               & LF
               & "        resize: (self: provider, p: ptr mut u8, old_size"
               & ": usize,"
               & LF
               & "                 new_size: usize, alignment: usize)"
               & LF
               & "                -> (moved: ptr mut u8) ! out_of_memory"
               & LF
               & "        free: (self: provider, p: ptr mut u8, size: usiz"
               & "e) -> none"
               & LF
               & "    end allocator"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A routine that adds to a list takes the list `inout` and"
               & " the allocator as an ordinary argument:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    record_reading: (provider: type is mem.allocator, in"
               & "out readings: vec.list(u32),"
               & LF
               & "                     memory: provider, value: u32) -> no"
               & "ne ! mem.out_of_memory ="
               & LF
               & "        try vec.push(readings, memory, value)"
               & LF
               & "    end record_reading"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The same `record_reading` runs with `heap.new()` as `mem"
               & "ory`, or with an arena over a buffer on the stack ([0820"
               & "])."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The list is changed, so it is passed `inout`. The alloca"
               & "tor is a service, so it is a handle: a small copyable va"
               & "lue, passed `in`, that refers to its provider's state. I"
               & "ts lifetime is checked once, where it is made from that "
               & "state, and using it locks nothing, so several containers"
               & " can allocate from one handle, and a region from its par"
               & "ent while both are live."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The allocator is passed to each call, not stored in the "
               & "container. A stored allocator would make the type `vec.l"
               & "ist(t, provider)`, so a list in an arena and a list on t"
               & "he heap would be different types and no function could t"
               & "ake both. Passing it keeps the type `vec.list(t)` and co"
               & "sts one argument at every call that can allocate."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The four entries:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "| entry | what it does |"
               & LF
               & "|---|---|"
               & LF
               & "| `alloc` | a new block, or `out_of_memory` |"
               & LF
               & "| `grow` | extend the block where it is; `false`, with n"
               & "othing changed, if it cannot |"
               & LF
               & "| `resize` | a new extent, larger or smaller, moving the"
               & " block if it must |"
               & LF
               & "| `free` | give the block back, with the size it was all"
               & "ocated with |"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "`resize` keeps the bytes the old and new extents share, "
               & "and a refusal reports `out_of_memory` with the block as "
               & "it was. A concept entry has no default, so a provider th"
               & "at can only move a block calls `mem.relocate`, which all"
               & "ocates, copies and frees through it."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The standard library's providers and containers:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "| module | what it gives |"
               & LF
               & "|---|---|"
               & LF
               & "| `core/arena` | a bump allocator over storage you suppl"
               & "y |"
               & LF
               & "| `core/pool` | uniform slots over storage you supply, f"
               & "reed slots reused |"
               & LF
               & "| `core/fault` | wraps any allocator and fails once a bu"
               & "dget of attempts is spent |"
               & LF
               & "| `hosted/heap` | the operating system's memory, really "
               & "freed, on every hosted system |"
               & LF
               & "| `core/vec` | a growable list |"
               & LF
               & "| `core/spill` | a list that keeps its first items inlin"
               & "e and spills to an allocator |"
               & LF
               & "| `core/map` | an open-addressed hash map |"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "None of them has a hidden heap or a fallback. Every fail"
               & "ure is `out_of_memory`, reported before anything changes"
               & ": a container that cannot grow keeps its old storage and"
               & " contents. The library guide lists each module's calls."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "When you use the containers directly:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- A map's key needs both an `equatable` and a `hashable`"
               & " conformance; the combined one does not supply its paren"
               & "t ([1340]). Equality must be an equivalence, equal keys "
               & "must hash alike, and neither may change while the key is"
               & " stored, including through a pointer inside the key. The"
               & " compiler does not check any of this."
               & LF
               & "- A map's entry cursor is just a position. Start a new w"
               & "alk after any change to the map, and never resume one on"
               & " a different map."
               & LF
               & "- A map's fields are public. Code that works below its o"
               & "perations must keep the map's invariants itself."
               & LF
               & "- A spill list's `spill.used` lends its items `from` the"
               & " list, so the list cannot spill or be released while tha"
               & "t view is still used ([0830]). Its first spill doubles t"
               & "he inline capacity, checking that the result fits `usize"
               & "`, or takes eight if the inline capacity is zero. If a s"
               & "pill or later growth fails, the list is unchanged."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "These are the providers' contracts. They do not make raw"
               & " storage safe: storage you hand an arena or a pool must "
               & "be live, aligned and as large as you say ([1720]).",
            when 1370 =>
               "`any C` holds a value of some type that satisfies concep"
               & "t C, decided at run time. It is two words: a pointer to "
               & "the data and a pointer to C's table of entries for that "
               & "type."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The pair is an ordinary copyable value. It can sit in a "
               & "variable, a field or a slice. What it points at is never"
               & " copied, because its size is unknown, so the pointee mus"
               & "t stay alive as long as any copy of the pair can use it."
               & " An arena ([0820]) is the usual place for it."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The pair needs no permission of its own: the concept's e"
               & "ntries carry it. An entry declared with `self: ptr mut t"
               & "` can only be satisfied by a pointer that may write, so "
               & "a stateful implementation behind `any` just works."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The pair does carry its pointee's origin ([0770]), so an"
               & " `any` over a local cannot go into a list that outlives "
               & "the frame."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "An `any C` is itself a provider of C and of every concep"
               & "t C builds on ([1340]), so a generic function ([1290]) t"
               & "akes one, and each entry it calls goes through the value"
               & "'s own table. Here an `any widget` is handed to a functi"
               & "on that wants a `drawable`:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    draw_on: (provider: type is drawable, item: ptr prov"
               & "ider,"
               & LF
               & "              target: ptr mut canvas) -> none ="
               & LF
               & "        provider.draw(item, target)"
               & LF
               & "    end draw_on"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    paint_one: (item: any widget, target: ptr mut canvas"
               & ") -> none ="
               & LF
               & "        draw_on(addr item, target)"
               & LF
               & "    end paint_one"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "It provides no other concept, and the compiler, not the "
               & "program, declares these conformances. An `any` is not er"
               & "ased again: where an `any C` is wanted, pass the one you"
               & " have rather than its address.",
            when 1380 =>
               "You build an `any` explicitly from a pointer. The concep"
               & "t comes from context where it can; otherwise name it."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    make_widget: (provider: type is mem.allocator, memor"
               & "y: provider)"
               & LF
               & "                 -> (item: any widget) ! mem.out_of_memo"
               & "ry ="
               & LF
               & "        initial: button = (text: ""OK"")"
               & LF
               & "        b := try mem.new(memory: memory, value: initial)"
               & LF
               & "        item = any(b)"
               & LF
               & "    end make_widget"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Here the result type supplies `widget`. Without a contex"
               & "t, `any(pointer)` needs exactly one concept the pointee'"
               & "s type conforms to."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A read-only pointer cannot build an `any` whose concept "
               & "has an entry taking `self: ptr mut t`. Once built, bindi"
               & "ng mutability controls only replacing the pair, not the "
               & "write authority its pointer already carries.",
            when 1390 =>
               "A call on an `any` looks up the entry in the table and p"
               & "asses the data pointer as the first argument. This is th"
               & "e only place in Landin that reads like a method call."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    paint: (items: []any widget, target: ptr mut canvas)"
               & " -> none ="
               & LF
               & "        for w in items do"
               & LF
               & "            w.draw(target)"
               & LF
               & "        end for"
               & LF
               & "    end paint"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "For an entry to be callable this way, its first paramete"
               & "r is `self` as `ptr t` or `ptr mut t`, or `self: t` take"
               & "n by value when `t` is a record, and the hidden type `t`"
               & " appears nowhere else in it. A by-value `self` is copied"
               & " out of the data pointer when the call is made. So to pu"
               & "t a small handle, such as an allocator handle `memory`, "
               & "behind `any`, take the address of the variable that hold"
               & "s it: `any(addr memory)`."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "`any C` is exactly C: a concept's parent gives no conver"
               & "sion, and two `any` types never convert to each other. T"
               & "he implicit `self` takes part in the same `from`, `escap"
               & "ing` and origin checks as a written pointer argument."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A call through `any C` is checked even if the program ha"
               & "s no conformance of C. Then no `any C` can be made and n"
               & "o value reaches the call, so it compiles to a trap that "
               & "never runs. A library that takes an erased capability th"
               & "erefore builds in a program that supplies none.",
            when 1400 =>
               "One slice can hold values of different types. A `[]t` is"
               & " always one `t`; a `[]any widget` holds buttons, labels "
               & "and anything else that is a widget.",
            when 1490 =>
               "`fixed` marks something the compiler knows before the pr"
               & "ogram runs. You meet it in three places: `fixed if`, whi"
               & "ch chooses declarations ([1500]); fixed parameters, whic"
               & "h are compile-time values ([1520]); and the fixed expres"
               & "sions that conditions, assertions and option defaults ar"
               & "e written in.",
            when 1520 =>
               "A `fixed` parameter is a value the caller supplies at co"
               & "mpile time, so it can size an array in the signature."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    make_buffer: (fixed capacity: u32, t: type is zeroab"
               & "le)"
               & LF
               & "                 -> (b: [capacity]t) = zeroed end"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Fixed parameters sit beside type parameters and work the"
               & " same way: the call supplies or deduces them, and they h"
               & "ave no storage at run time ([1290]).",
            when 1500 =>
               "`fixed if` picks which module-level declarations exist i"
               & "n this build. It works on declarations only, not on stat"
               & "ements."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    fixed if compiler.arch == arm64 then"
               & LF
               & "        word_bits: u32 = 64"
               & LF
               & "    elsif compiler.arch == cortex_m0 then"
               & LF
               & "        word_bits: u32 = 32"
               & LF
               & "    end if"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Every arm is parsed, so a syntax error in an arm you are"
               & " not building is still an error. Only the selected arm's"
               & " declarations join the module; the others have no names,"
               & " no types and no effect. Arms may nest and may be empty,"
               & " and an arm opens no scope."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The compiler supplies these facts:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "| fact | what it says |"
               & LF
               & "|---|---|"
               & LF
               & "| `compiler.arch` | `x86_64`, `arm64`, `rv64`, `cortex_m"
               & "0` or `synthetic_32` |"
               & LF
               & "| `compiler.os` | `linux`, `darwin`, `freebsd` or `frees"
               & "tanding` |"
               & LF
               & "| `compiler.word_size` | the width of `usize`, in bits |"
               & LF
               & "| `compiler.byte_order` | `little` or `big` |"
               & LF
               & "| `compiler.build_mode` | `debug`, the default, or `rele"
               & "ase` |"
               & LF
               & "| `compiler.c_sysv_lp64`, `compiler.c_darwin_lp64`, `com"
               & "piler.c_aapcs64_lp64`, `compiler.c_riscv_lp64d` | which "
               & "C ABI the target uses, each true for its own ABI alone |"
               & LF
               & "| `compiler.c_runtime` | whether C's runtime starts the "
               & "program and its library is linked |"
               & LF
               & "| `compiler.feature.NAME` | whether the selected CPU lev"
               & "el has feature NAME |"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Ask the C ABI question directly rather than guessing it "
               & "from the architecture: Linux and Apple arm64 share `comp"
               & "iler.arch == arm64` and answer differently here. `compil"
               & "er.os` is separate from the C ABI because Linux and Free"
               & "BSD share a calling convention on each architecture but "
               & "not every libc record layout."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "`compiler.c_runtime` is always true on Darwin and FreeBS"
               & "D, true on Linux only when the build asks with `--link=l"
               & "ibc`, and never true on firmware. A library calls the C "
               & "library only where this says it is there."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A build assumes a CPU feature level, chosen with `--leve"
               & "l=` and defaulting to the oldest processor of the archit"
               & "ecture. `compiler.feature.NAME` asks about one feature, "
               & "such as `bmi2` on x86-64, `lse` on arm64, `idiv` on the "
               & "M profile or `xtheadba` on RV64. A feature of another ar"
               & "chitecture is simply false, so you need no `compiler.arc"
               & "h` test first:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    fixed if compiler.feature.idiv then"
               & LF
               & "        divide_cycles: u32 = 12"
               & LF
               & "    else"
               & LF
               & "        divide_cycles: u32 = 40"
               & LF
               & "    end if"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A level changes which instructions the build may use, ne"
               & "ver a layout or a calling convention, so code built at t"
               & "wo levels links together. `spec.md` lists the levels and"
               & " their features."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "When only a value differs, you need no `fixed if` at all"
               & ". A module value may read a fact, and a routine branches"
               & " on that value with an ordinary `if`:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    fast_count: bool = compiler.feature.popcnt"
               & LF
               & "    on_linux: bool = compiler.os == linux"
               & LF
               & "    word_bytes: usize = compiler.word_size / 8"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "An integer fact takes its type from context. The atom fa"
               & "cts, `compiler.arch`, `compiler.os`, `compiler.byte_orde"
               & "r` and `compiler.build_mode`, are read by comparing them"
               & " with one of their atoms."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A routine body reads no fact directly, and `fixed if` ca"
               & "nnot begin a statement. What differs by value branches o"
               & "n a module value like those above; what needs a call tha"
               & "t one system lacks is a routine declared in each arm of "
               & "a module-level `fixed if`."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Fine print:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- Build mode is a value you ask for. It does not change "
               & "runtime checks or optimization."
               & LF
               & "- Conditions may also read your own options [1530]."
               & LF
               & "- A condition is a closed fold: literals, parentheses, u"
               & "nary `-`, integer `+ - * / %` without overflow, integer "
               & "comparisons, equality, `not`, `and`, `or`, and `sizeof` "
               & "and `alignof` of the built-in scalar types. Sizes count "
               & "target bytes, so `compiler.word_size == 8 * sizeof usize"
               & "` holds on 32-bit and 64-bit targets alike."
               & LF
               & "- Calls, runtime names, measurements of your own types a"
               & "nd aggregate operations are refused, even in an operand "
               & "that short-circuiting would skip."
               & LF
               & "- There is no `public fixed if`, no trailing name after "
               & "`end if`, and no `fixed if` inside a block, struct, sign"
               & "ature or template."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Exact rules: spec [1940].",
            when 1510 =>
               "`compiler.assert` stops the build when a fixed condition"
               & " is false."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    compiler.assert(sizeof usize == 8)"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "It is a module-level directive, allowed in a selected `f"
               & "ixed if` arm too, and an assertion in an inactive arm do"
               & "es nothing. The condition is the same closed fold as [15"
               & "00] and must be a bool; a false one is reported at its o"
               & "wn source. An assertion may also measure your own types,"
               & " as in `compiler.assert(sizeof header == 8)`, and is the"
               & "n checked once the type is laid out."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "It is a builtin call rather than a keyword, like the res"
               & "t of [1560]. That leaves the word `assert` free for an o"
               & "rdinary routine of your own, such as the `assert` of [10"
               & "40].",
            when 1530 =>
               "An `option` declares a typed build switch with a default"
               & ", which the build can override. A misspelt switch is a c"
               & "ompile error, never a silent false."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    option log_level: u32 = 0"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Read it in a fixed condition, and set it on the command "
               & "line with `--option=log_level=2`:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    fixed if log_level > 1 then"
               & LF
               & "        trace_enabled: bool = true"
               & LF
               & "    else"
               & LF
               & "        trace_enabled: bool = false"
               & LF
               & "    end if"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "What to know:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- An option's type is `bool` or a built-in integer type;"
               & " `usize` and `isize` take the selected target's bounds. "
               & "It has no storage at run time, and its name is visible o"
               & "nly in fixed conditions, option defaults and compiler as"
               & "sertions."
               & LF
               & "- Option names are global across the whole program, so e"
               & "ach is declared exactly once, unconditionally at module "
               & "level and never inside a `fixed if` arm: the compiler co"
               & "llects every option before it chooses arms. An option ma"
               & "y not share a name with an active module declaration, an"
               & " import or a compiler-owned atom such as `arm64` or `rel"
               & "ease`."
               & LF
               & "- A default may refer to another option, even one declar"
               & "ed later, and sees that option's overridden value. Cycle"
               & "s are refused, and every default must be valid even when"
               & " overridden."
               & LF
               & "- `--option=NAME=VALUE` takes `true` or `false`, or sign"
               & "ed decimal integer text. Unknown or repeated names, malf"
               & "ormed values and values out of range are errors."
               & LF
               & "- `--build-mode=debug` or `--build-mode=release` sets th"
               & "e separate built-in build mode.",
            when 1540 =>
               "There are no compile-time loops and no compile-time func"
               & "tion calls. Nothing of yours runs while the program is b"
               & "eing built."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The builtin modules of [1560] look like an exception and"
               & " are not one: their members are operations the compiler "
               & "recognizes, not your functions run during the build, and"
               & " nobody can add another. Some of their inputs must be fi"
               & "xed, such as assertion conditions, library names and ass"
               & "embly text; others, such as atomic data and assembly inp"
               & "ut operands, can be runtime values."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "When you need a generated table or a structure-of-arrays"
               & " layout, write a program that writes the source, and hav"
               & "e the build run it.",
            when 1550 =>
               "`refine` compiles the whole program at once into assembl"
               & "y text, and the platform's own assembler and linker turn"
               & " that into an executable. There is no LLVM and no C in b"
               & "etween."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- The emitted assembly is deterministic: the same progra"
               & "m and options give the same bytes, whatever the build di"
               & "rectory, environment or order. The linked executable is "
               & "not covered by that promise."
               & LF
               & "- Every ordinary routine and every interrupt handler set"
               & "s up the frame pointer, so a debugger or a backtrace can"
               & " always walk the stack. On Cortex-M0 a routine with no c"
               & "alls, no assembly and no stack changes may leave it alon"
               & "e (spec [1990]). A naked routine ([1570]) owns its frame"
               & " instead."
               & LF
               & "- The whole program is compiled together ([1480]). The c"
               & "ompiler may keep private caches, but there is no stable "
               & "separate-compilation interface."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Going through C would lose the calling convention, the t"
               & "raps and the debug information Landin is precise about, "
               & "so every target's backend is Landin's own. The README li"
               & "sts the targets."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "On Cortex-M the `cortex_m0` backend emits ARMv6-M Thumb,"
               & " the default `armv6-m` level. `--level=armv7-m` or `--le"
               & "vel=armv7e-m` lets it use that level's instructions too,"
               & " which the processor must then support. All three levels"
               & " are little-endian and soft-float. A firmware request em"
               & "its its own reset code, vector table and linker script: "
               & "reset copies initialized data and RAM code out of flash "
               & "and clears BSS before your code runs."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Exact rules: spec [1990].",
            when 1560 =>
               "Three builtin modules reach the toolchain. They belong t"
               & "o the reserved `landin` package (`landin/compiler` and i"
               & "ts siblings) and are in scope without an import, which i"
               & "s why nobody else can use their bare names."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "| module | what it reaches |"
               & LF
               & "| --- | --- |"
               & LF
               & "| `compiler` | target, word size, byte order, build mode"
               & ", CPU features, the atomic, volatile and register intrin"
               & "sics, the two stack builtins under fibres ([1675]), and "
               & "`compiler.unreachable()`, a call that never returns and "
               & "stops the program as a failed check does |"
               & LF
               & "| `assembler` | inline assembly |"
               & LF
               & "| `linker` | libraries, sections, entry |"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Their members are built into the compiler; you cannot wr"
               & "ite one yourself. Some arguments must be fixed when the "
               & "program is compiled: `compiler.assert` takes a fixed con"
               & "dition, `linker.library` a fixed name and `assembler.blo"
               & "ck` fixed instruction text. Atomic and volatile pointer "
               & "and value operands, and assembly inputs, can be runtime "
               & "values; an atomic's ordering is always a fixed `compiler"
               & "` atom."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "What works today:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- Every hosted target has the compiler facts, `compiler."
               & "assert`, `linker.library` ([1590]) and the scalar atomic"
               & "s and barriers ([1620])."
               & LF
               & "- Cortex-M0 also has placement annotations ([1640]) and "
               & "the firmware entry request ([1650]), which hosted target"
               & "s refuse."
               & LF
               & "- `assembler.block` is checked on every target ([1630])."
               & LF
               & "- Any other tool operation is refused by name."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Something is builtin when the compiler has to understand"
               & " it. Atomics are, because opaque assembly in a hot loop "
               & "wrecks the register allocation around it. Masking interr"
               & "upts is not: a critical section wants nothing moved acro"
               & "ss it, which is exactly what opaque assembly gives. So `"
               & "cortex_m.disable_interrupts` and its kin are an ordinary"
               & " module per target, written with `assembler.block` ([163"
               & "0]). An operation that comes to mean the same thing on e"
               & "very target, as the atomics do, can move into `compiler`"
               & ". There are no vector intrinsics: fixed arrays are the v"
               & "ector type and take element-wise operators ([0590]).",
            when 1580 =>
               "A bodyless `extern(c)` declaration imports a C function."
               & " `refine` reads Landin, never C headers: a separate, det"
               & "erministic binding generator reads headers and writes th"
               & "ese declarations for you."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    none_returned: atom"
               & LF
               & "    allocation: type = none_returned | ptr mut u8"
               & LF
               & "    extern(c) malloc: (n: usize) -> (p: allocation)"
               & LF
               & "    extern(c) free: (p: ptr mut u8) -> none"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A C pointer may be null and a Landin pointer may not. So"
               & " a C result or parameter that may be null is [0480]'s on"
               & "e-atom pointer union, which costs no extra bits, and you"
               & " `match` the result before using the pointer. `ptr(0)` i"
               & "s refused, and so is any fixed expression that folds to "
               & "zero at the target's `usize` width. A pointer built from"
               & " an integer at run time is checked, and zero traps, even"
               & " in `unchecked`: losing the origin is not permission to "
               & "make null. Absent allocator backing is a named union too"
               & ", never a fake `ptr(1)`."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A header does not say everything Landin wants to know: w"
               & "hether a pointer may be null, who owns it, what it is `f"
               & "rom`, whether C retains it, how to read incoming varargs"
               & ". The generator takes that from a written policy, not fr"
               & "om a handwritten replacement signature."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A function found while the program runs, such as a symbo"
               & "l of a loaded library, is just an address. Apply a C fun"
               & "ction type to it and you have a function value:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    c_adder: type = extern(c) (a: i32, b: i32) -> (r: i3"
               & "2)"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    add_at: (address: usize) -> (sum: i32) ="
               & LF
               & "        add := c_adder(address)"
               & LF
               & "        sum = add(2, 3)"
               & LF
               & "    end add_at"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Nothing can check that code of that type is there, as no"
               & "thing checks a C cast, and the value is yours to call on"
               & "ly while the library stays loaded."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Four C ABIs are supported: SysV AMD64 LP64 on Linux and "
               & "FreeBSD x86-64, Apple's arm64 variant of AAPCS64 on macO"
               & "S, the standard AAPCS64 on Linux and FreeBSD arm64, and "
               & "RV64 LP64D on Linux RV64."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "`platform/c` names C's types: the signed and unsigned in"
               & "teger widths, `c_size`, `c_ptrdiff`, `c_float`, `c_doubl"
               & "e` and `c_bool`. It asserts `compiler.c_sysv_lp64 or com"
               & "piler.c_darwin_lp64 or compiler.c_aapcs64_lp64 or compil"
               & "er.c_riscv_lp64d`, so it admits exactly those four ABIs;"
               & " another target with the same pointer width is not admit"
               & "ted. Plain `c_char` is the ABI's plain `char`, a number "
               & "and not a Unicode scalar: `i8` under SysV and Apple's AB"
               & "I, `u8` under the standard AAPCS64 and RV64."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "What crosses the boundary:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "| kind | how it crosses |"
               & LF
               & "| --- | --- |"
               & LF
               & "| integers, `bool`, pointers, `f32`, `f64`, C function v"
               & "alues | by value |"
               & LF
               & "| nonempty `layout(c)` structs, with nested structs, non"
               & "empty fixed arrays and fixed C callbacks as fields | by "
               & "value |"
               & LF
               & "| packed records and records with a flexible array membe"
               & "r | by pointer only |"
               & LF
               & "| fixed arrays | as fields or pointees, never as a by-va"
               & "lue argument or result |"
               & LF
               & "| C enums | as their integer values |"
               & LF
               & "| unions, bitfields, globals, thread-locals, nullable ca"
               & "llbacks | through the generator's representations and ad"
               & "apters |"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Anything else a header can say, such as `long double`, 1"
               & "28-bit integers, complex or vector types, or forwarding "
               & "a `va_list`, is refused by name rather than guessed."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A variadic C function writes `, ...` after at least one "
               & "fixed parameter. This `...` is C's variadic tail, unrela"
               & "ted to [0960]'s `! ...`."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    extern(c) printf: (format: ptr u8, ...) -> (written:"
               & " i32)"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    say_count: (format: ptr u8, count: i32) -> none ="
               & LF
               & "        _ = printf(format, count, 1.5)"
               & LF
               & "    end say_count"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- Calls, direct or through a function value, are positio"
               & "nal only, including the fixed parameters."
               & LF
               & "- The variadic tail takes scalars, pointers and fixed C "
               & "callbacks, not structs, arrays, slices, atoms or `any`."
               & LF
               & "- C's promotions apply: `f32` becomes `f64`, and `bool` "
               & "and narrow integers become C `int`. An untyped literal i"
               & "s `i32` or `f64`."
               & LF
               & "- A variadic function value may be stored and called in "
               & "Landin, but it cannot fill a C callback field or paramet"
               & "er."
               & LF
               & "- A Landin definition cannot receive varargs; the genera"
               & "tor writes a C adapter from an explicit extraction schem"
               & "a instead."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Exact rules: spec [1975].",
            when 1600 =>
               "Give an `extern(c)` declaration a body and you have defi"
               & "ned a C function in Landin. Make it `public` and C code "
               & "can call it by its name."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    public extern(c) my_add: (a: i32, b: i32) -> (r: i32"
               & ") ="
               & LF
               & "        r = a + b"
               & LF
               & "    end my_add"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "`extern(c)` chooses the calling convention and nothing e"
               & "lse. Without a body it imports; with a body it defines; "
               & "`public` is what exports, making the declared name a glo"
               & "bal symbol. A private C definition is still useful: its "
               & "address can be handed to C as a callback."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- A C signature is not generic, takes only `in` paramete"
               & "rs and returns at most one value."
               & LF
               & "- Landin's declared failures do not cross. Recover insid"
               & "e the body and return the status or value the C interfac"
               & "e promises."
               & LF
               & "- Nothing is promised if a foreign exception or a `longj"
               & "mp` crosses Landin frames."
               & LF
               & "- Cortex-M has no C signatures yet. Its link names and p"
               & "lacement are enabled separately ([1610], [1640]).",
            when 1610 =>
               "`link(symbol: ""..."")` gives a declaration a linker name "
               & "its Landin name cannot spell, or does not want. It follo"
               & "ws `extern(c)` for a C import or definition, or stands a"
               & "lone before a native Landin function or a module datum."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    link(symbol: ""native_identity"") identity: (value: i3"
               & "2) -> (result: i32) ="
               & LF
               & "        result = value"
               & LF
               & "    end identity"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    extern(c) link(symbol: ""foreign_add"") add: (a: i32, "
               & "b: i32) -> (r: i32)"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Landin code still calls these `identity` and `add`: `lin"
               & "k` changes neither lookup nor signature nor Landin visib"
               & "ility. The standalone form keeps the native Landin conve"
               & "ntion and still needs `= body end`; `link` never means C"
               & " and never means bodyless."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "It does export. A link name is a global symbol whether o"
               & "r not the declaration is `public`, and that is how you e"
               & "xport a module datum deliberately. Without `link`, C imp"
               & "orts and public C definitions use their declared name. E"
               & "very other routine and datum, public or private, is a lo"
               & "cal symbol under its own name, so no C library can colli"
               & "de with it. It is renamed, with its module path, only wh"
               & "en the program holds that name twice."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- A link name matches `[A-Za-z_.$][A-Za-z0-9_.$]*`: no w"
               & "hitespace, no `@` suffix, no assembler expression."
               & LF
               & "- It is the name before the platform's prefix. ELF uses "
               & "it unchanged; Darwin adds one underscore, so `foreign_ad"
               & "d` becomes `_foreign_add` and `_entry` becomes `__entry`"
               & ". The backend quotes it where the assembler needs that. "
               & "Native and C link names follow the same rule."
               & LF
               & "- Bodyless C declarations may share a symbol with each o"
               & "ther and with one definition if their signatures agree. "
               & "Disagreeing signatures and a second definition are refus"
               & "ed. `escaping` counts, although C has no such word: an i"
               & "mport of a C function that keeps its pointer must say so"
               & ", as the definition does."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Exact rules: spec [1975].",
            when 1615 =>
               "`link(thread)` before a module `mut` binding gives every"
               & " thread its own instance of it. A thread reads and write"
               & "s only its own, and each one starts at zero when its thr"
               & "ead starts."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    link(thread) mut last_failure: i32"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    note_failure: (number: i32) -> none ="
               & LF
               & "        last_failure = number"
               & LF
               & "    end note_failure"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Two threads calling `note_failure` at once each keep the"
               & "ir own number, so this needs no lock. `hosted/system` ke"
               & "eps each thread's last errno this way."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- There is no initial value: write none, and use a type "
               & "that has a zero, so not a pointer or a slice."
               & LF
               & "- It takes no `symbol` or `section`, so a C thread-local"
               & " is not named this way; use the binding generator ([1580"
               & "])."
               & LF
               & "- On a microcontroller, which runs one thread, it is ord"
               & "inary module storage."
               & LF
               & "- Its address is good while its thread runs. Do not hand"
               & " it to a thread that may outlive the one it belongs to."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Exact rules: spec [1996].",
            when 1590 =>
               "`linker.library` asks the C driver for a library, as `-l"
               & "NAME` asks it in C. Write it next to the declarations th"
               & "at need it, not in a separate build file. `linker.direct"
               & "ory` says where to look, relative to the file it is in."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    linker.library(""raylib"")"
               & LF
               & "    linker.library(""m"")"
               & LF
               & "    linker.directory(""vendor/lib"")"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "This links `libraylib` from `vendor/lib` beside the sour"
               & "ce, and `libm` from wherever the C driver keeps it."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- The name is a fixed text literal of ASCII letters, dig"
               & "its, `_`, `-` and `.`. It is nonempty, does not begin wi"
               & "th `-` and is not only dots. The driver takes a shared l"
               & "ibrary or an archive of that name, as it would for a C p"
               & "rogram."
               & LF
               & "- Active directives reach the linker after the program, "
               & "in source order, repeats included. So moving one among t"
               & "he module's declarations can change how archives resolve"
               & ". This is the one place where declaration order matters."
               & " A directive in an inactive arm of a `fixed if` ([1500])"
               & " adds nothing."
               & LF
               & "- A directory is a fixed, nonempty text literal: relativ"
               & "e to the directory of the file that names it, or absolut"
               & "e. One that does not exist is refused before anything is"
               & " linked. `-L DIR` on `refine compile`, `run` or `check` "
               & "names another, relative to where the build runs, and is "
               & "searched first, so a build can put its own copy of a lib"
               & "rary ahead. Each directory is also where the program loo"
               & "ks for a shared library when it starts."
               & LF
               & "- A library the link cannot find is named with every dir"
               & "ectory searched."
               & LF
               & "- On Linux a library links beside the C library, so a bu"
               & "ild that asks for one needs `--link=libc`; the default s"
               & "tatic link ([1650]) refuses it, and refuses a directory "
               & "too."
               & LF
               & "- Cortex-M firmware refuses `linker.library`, `linker.di"
               & "rectory` and `-L`.",
            when 1570 =>
               "A function's calling convention is written `extern(...)`"
               & " before its name. Without one, a function uses Landin's "
               & "own convention."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "| convention | for |"
               & LF
               & "| --- | --- |"
               & LF
               & "| `extern(c)` | the target's C ABI ([1580], [1600]) |"
               & LF
               & "| `extern(interrupt)` | an interrupt handler, whose entr"
               & "y, exit and vector placement differ from a call ([1640])"
               & " |"
               & LF
               & "| `extern(naked)` | no prologue at all: the body is one "
               & "assembly block ([1630]) |"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The convention is an atom, not a keyword, so the set can"
               & " grow, for example to `extern(aapcs)`, `extern(sysv)` or"
               & " `extern(win64)`. No other convention is in Landin yet."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The two machine conventions are their own function types"
               & ":"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- A definition is nongeneric, exactly `() -> none`, and "
               & "declares no failures. Its function value can be stored o"
               & "r named by a vector, but not called or converted to an o"
               & "rdinary or C function."
               & LF
               & "- An interrupt handler runs an ordinary Landin frame on "
               & "top of the hardware's exception frame and returns throug"
               & "h EXC_RETURN."
               & LF
               & "- A naked body is one assembly block with no compiler pr"
               & "ologue. The stack, registers, calls and return instructi"
               & "on are yours, and falling off the end traps."
               & LF
               & "- Neither convention keeps a routine from section garbag"
               & "e collection ([1640])."
               & LF
               & "- Naked routines also work on hosted x86-64, arm64 and R"
               & "V64; `platform/linux`'s process entry is one.",
            when 1620 =>
               "Atomic operations are `compiler` builtins, not assembly,"
               & " so the compiler knows which memory they touch and can s"
               & "till allocate registers around them. The ordering is a f"
               & "ixed atom."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    bump: (p: ptr mut u32) -> none ="
               & LF
               & "        _ = compiler.atomic_add(p, 1, compiler.acq_rel)"
               & LF
               & "    end bump"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "`core/atomic` wraps one `u8`, `u16`, `u32` or `u64` in a"
               & " cell whose operations carry their orderings in their na"
               & "mes: `load` acquires, `store` releases, `exchange`, `add"
               & "` and `compare_exchange` do both, and `load_relaxed`, `s"
               & "tore_relaxed` and `add_relaxed` order nothing else."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    import core/atomic"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    mut ticks: atomic.cell(u32) = zeroed"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    count_tick: () -> none ="
               & LF
               & "        _ = atomic.sole_add(addr ticks, 1)"
               & LF
               & "    end count_tick"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Cortex-M0 has no read-modify-write atomics at all, so th"
               & "ere a cell has no `exchange`, `add` or `compare_exchange"
               & "`, and no `u64` cell, and the compiler says so where you"
               & " call one. What it has is the single writer's add, `sole"
               & "_add`: a load and a store, correct while one context alo"
               & "ne, an interrupt handler say, writes the cell. State sev"
               & "eral contexts write takes `platform/cortex_m`'s critical"
               & " section instead."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Each tool for shared memory does one thing:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "| tool | what it gives |"
               & LF
               & "| --- | --- |"
               & LF
               & "| atomics | synchronization of coherent CPU memory betwe"
               & "en threads |"
               & LF
               & "| volatile accesses ([0850]) | every access happens, as "
               & "written; no synchronization between threads |"
               & LF
               & "| compiler barrier | the compiler forgets what it knew a"
               & "bout memory; no hardware ordering |"
               & LF
               & "| the target's device and completion barriers | hardware"
               & " ordering and completion of the accesses before them |"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A DMA buffer stays an ordinary slice. When the device ha"
               & "s finished, by its own protocol and not merely because a"
               & "n interrupt arrived, make its writes visible with the ta"
               & "rget's barriers or cache maintenance and a compiler barr"
               & "ier, then read the buffer. Masking interrupts does not s"
               & "top DMA."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "`spec.md` defines the supported operations, the exact or"
               & "derings, alignment traps, happens-before and what a race"
               & " may do. Exact rules: spec [1990].",
            when 1630 =>
               "`assembler.block` takes fixed quoted or raw text in a ro"
               & "utine body, and after it the operands that carry values "
               & "in and out. An operand names its direction, its name, it"
               & "s type and the register it is placed `at`:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    disable_interrupts: () -> (previous: u32) ="
               & LF
               & "        previous = assembler.block(""mrs {mask}, primask\"
               & "ncpsid i"","
               & LF
               & "                                   out mask: u32 at gene"
               & "ral)"
               & LF
               & "    end disable_interrupts"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    restore_interrupts: (previous: u32) -> none ="
               & LF
               & "        assembler.block(""msr primask, {mask}\nisb sy"","
               & LF
               & "                        in mask: u32 at general = previo"
               & "us)"
               & LF
               & "    end restore_interrupts"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A register is a name the target answers for, never text."
               & " `general` asks the compiler to choose one, and `{mask}`"
               & " in the text is where the chosen register goes, spelled "
               & "at the width the operand's type selects. A fixed registe"
               & "r is the architecture's own full-width name, and the tex"
               & "t may use it directly. `in` carries a value in, `out` ca"
               & "rries one out, and `inout` does both through one registe"
               & "r. The outputs are the block's value, as a function's na"
               & "med returns are its: one is a scalar, several are an ano"
               & "nymous result you destructure."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    (low, high) := assembler.block(""rdtsc"", out low: u32"
               & " at rax,"
               & LF
               & "                                   out high: u32 at rdx)"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "What an operand can be:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- An integer scalar that fits one register. A float oper"
               & "and is refused by name. Float operands are not in Landin"
               & " yet; they are planned (Language evolution, in the roadm"
               & "ap)."
               & LF
               & "- A pointer crosses as `usize(p)` and comes back through"
               & " `ptr(u)`, whose zero check then happens there. A flag c"
               & "omes back as `x <> 0`."
               & LF
               & "- Inputs are evaluated once, left to right, before the b"
               & "lock runs."
               & LF
               & "- The integer keeps no permission. So a routine around a"
               & " block that writes storage takes it as `ptr mut u8` or `"
               & "[]mut u8`: its callers are checked against that paramete"
               & "r, and an immutable buffer is refused there rather than "
               & "handed to the system."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "What a block may touch: every block may overwrite the re"
               & "gisters a call may, and the flags. A register a call mus"
               & "t preserve is declared, as an operand or as a discarded "
               & "output `out _ at rbx`, and that is what makes the routin"
               & "e save it. The frame, stack and link registers are never"
               & " named, so a block cannot break a backtrace. The languag"
               & "e reserves no register of its own."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Every block is opaque to the compiler: memory knowledge "
               & "is invalidated and memory accesses cannot be moved acros"
               & "s it, which is exactly what a critical section wants. It"
               & " is not itself a hardware barrier. An ordinary block is "
               & "straight-line code, with no labels or calls; a system ca"
               & "ll returns to the next instruction, so it is straight-li"
               & "ne too. The older Cortex-M0 form stays as a shorthand: `"
               & "assembler.block(text, value)` passes one `u32` in and ou"
               & "t of r0, and the text names r0 itself."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "`platform/cortex_m` wraps the common blocks:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- `disable_interrupts()` returns the prior PRIMASK, and "
               & "`restore_interrupts(previous)` puts it back, so nested c"
               & "ritical sections do not enable interrupts too early. Bot"
               & "h work in thread and handler mode. They do not stop DMA "
               & "or mask NMI or HardFault."
               & LF
               & "- `wait_for_interrupt()` issues DSB, then WFI. Waking up"
               & " is a reason to check the device again, not proof that i"
               & "t finished."
               & LF
               & "- `compiler_barrier`, `device_barrier` and `completion_b"
               & "arrier` are the three distinct boundaries of [1620]. The"
               & "re is no cache or scheduler API."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A naked body ([1570]) owns its control flow, so its bloc"
               & "k may use labels and branches. This entry runs after the"
               & " compiler's reset has initialized data and stacks:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    extern(naked) start: () -> none ="
               & LF
               & "        assembler.block("""""""
               & LF
               & "            1:"
               & LF
               & "            wfi"
               & LF
               & "            b 1b"
               & LF
               & "            """""")"
               & LF
               & "    end start"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    refine compile main.ldn --target=cortex-m0 --firmwar"
               & "e-entry=start --emit=exe"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The request names the source entry. A naked entry may se"
               & "t up PSP or call explicitly linked ordinary code, but it"
               & " must then meet that code's eight-byte stack alignment, "
               & "r9 and frame obligations. `none` is not a promise never "
               & "to return: the compiler traps if the entry returns or th"
               & "e naked block falls through. There is no hosted caller t"
               & "o return to."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Exact rules, with each target's registers, the text rule"
               & "s and your obligations: spec [1990].",
            when 1640 =>
               "On Cortex-M, a `link(...)` annotation also places a decl"
               & "aration: in a section, at an alignment, in an interrupt "
               & "vector slot, and kept against the linker's section garba"
               & "ge collection."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    link(vector: 16)"
               & LF
               & "    extern(interrupt) device_handler: () -> none ="
               & LF
               & "        assembler.block(""nop"")"
               & LF
               & "    end device_handler"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    link(section: "".rodata.firmware_mark"", align: 16, ke"
               & "ep)"
               & LF
               & "    firmware_mark: [4]u8 = [55, 48, 49, 0]"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Slot 16 is IRQ0 in the selected profile: a vector number"
               & " is the absolute slot in the table, not an IRQ number or"
               & " a peripheral's name."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The compiler owns the vector table. Its first word is th"
               & "e initial stack pointer, its second the compiler's reset"
               & ", reserved slots are zero, and `vector` annotations fill"
               & " in handlers with their types checked. So a stack addres"
               & "s, a reserved word and a handler can never be mixed up. "
               & "A vector annotation needs a firmware-entry request ([165"
               & "0])."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "What survives the link:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- The table is kept and refers to each handler in it, so"
               & " those handlers survive."
               & LF
               & "- An interrupt routine nothing refers to and nothing kee"
               & "ps can disappear."
               & LF
               & "- `keep` retains the section that holds the declaration."
               & " Several objects placed in one section share that retent"
               & "ion."
               & LF
               & "- A link name alone retains nothing."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Where things go:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "| what | sections |"
               & LF
               & "| --- | --- |"
               & LF
               & "| functions | `.text.*` in flash, or `.ramtext.*`, copie"
               & "d to RAM at reset |"
               & LF
               & "| immutable data | `.rodata.*` |"
               & LF
               & "| mutable data | `.data.*`, or `.bss.*` when zeroed |"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- `align` is a power of two up to 256. It places the obj"
               & "ect and does not change its type's layout."
               & LF
               & "- `align` and `vector` take decimal literals; digit sepa"
               & "rators are fine, hex and expressions are refused."
               & LF
               & "- The image is 32 KiB of flash and 16 KiB of RAM, whose "
               & "top 4 KiB is reserved for stacks. The linker checks boun"
               & "ds and overlap."
               & LF
               & "- The compiler refuses reserved vectors, a section that "
               & "does not match the declaration, duplicate attributes and"
               & " symbol collisions."
               & LF
               & "- There is no module initialization in user code ([1460]"
               & ")."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Calling convention, symbol name, placement and retention"
               & " are four independent choices; none implies another.",
            when 730 =>
               "A hardware register mixes kinds of field, and confusing "
               & "them is the classic driver bug. Landin describes a regis"
               & "ter as a packed struct whose fields sit at bit positions"
               & " you write down."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "| kind | what it is | how you write it |"
               & LF
               & "|---|---|---|"
               & LF
               & "| flag | one bit | `bool` |"
               & LF
               & "| selection | one of several named values, encoded as th"
               & "e datasheet says | a union of atoms with encodings |"
               & LF
               & "| set | several independent flags at once | a packed str"
               & "uct of `bool` |"
               & LF
               & "| number | a plain number | an unsigned field such as `u"
               & "12` |"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The examples below use these atoms:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    irq_rx: atom"
               & LF
               & "    irq_tx: atom"
               & LF
               & "    irq_err: atom"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    internal: atom"
               & LF
               & "    external: atom"
               & LF
               & "    pll: atom"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    active_low, active_high: atom"
               & LF
               & "    by_1, by_2: atom"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A selection is a union that gives each atom its encoding"
               & ". The encoding belongs to the union, not to the atom, so"
               & " the same atom may be encoded differently in another reg"
               & "ister. The width is the smallest that holds the largest "
               & "encoding; write a base type only when the datasheet give"
               & "s the field more room than it needs."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    polarity: type = (active_low = 0 | active_high = 1) "
               & "-- one bit"
               & LF
               & "    clock_mode: type = (internal = 0 | external = 1 | pl"
               & "l = 4)"
               & LF
               & "    divider_sel: type = u4 (by_1 = 0 | by_2 = 1) -- four"
               & " bits, as given"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A set is a packed struct of `bool`, one field per member"
               & ", each at the bit its member's encoding names. Membershi"
               & "p is a field read, adding or removing a member is a fiel"
               & "d write, and building one is an ordinary struct literal "
               & "with `of` ([0720]). There is no set literal or set opera"
               & "tor, because a struct needs none. Inside a larger image "
               & "the same fields sit at the image's bits, offset by where"
               & " the set begins."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    irq: type = (irq_rx = 0 | irq_tx = 1 | irq_err = 2)"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    irq_set: type = layout(packed, u32) struct"
               & LF
               & "        irq_rx: bool at 0"
               & LF
               & "        irq_tx: bool at 1"
               & LF
               & "        irq_err: bool at 2"
               & LF
               & "    end irq_set"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    control: type = layout(packed) struct"
               & LF
               & "        enable: bool at 0"
               & LF
               & "        mode: clock_mode at 4..6"
               & LF
               & "        irq_rx: bool at 8 -- irq's encodings, from bit 8"
               & LF
               & "        irq_tx: bool at 9"
               & LF
               & "        irq_err: bool at 10"
               & LF
               & "        divider: u12 at 16..27"
               & LF
               & "    end control"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A set earns its place when its members mean something. A"
               & " numbered bank of lines does not: sixteen output pins ar"
               & "e `[16]bool` at `0..15`, not sixteen invented atoms."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Bit positions are written, never implied by field order,"
               & " so reordering the fields of a published register is not"
               & " a hidden breaking change. In a packed struct every fiel"
               & "d carries a position, or none does. Any bit no field cla"
               & "ims is reserved: you cannot name it, no completion list "
               & "offers it, and a read-modify-write leaves it untouched."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "You rarely write these by hand. A generator reading a ve"
               & "ndor's SVD file writes the fields, and the helpers [0740"
               & "] uses, for every register. SVD generation belongs to th"
               & "e companion tool; the language has no `set(X)` type form"
               & "er to do it instead."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A field may be an array, which is how a real peripheral "
               & "packs sixteen two-bit pin settings into one word. The el"
               & "ement width times the count must equal the range, and el"
               & "ement zero takes the low bits."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    pin_input, pin_output, pin_alternate, pin_analog: at"
               & "om"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    pin_mode: type = (pin_input = 0 | pin_output = 1 | p"
               & "in_alternate = 2 | pin_analog = 3)"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    moder: type = layout(packed) struct"
               & LF
               & "        pins: [16]pin_mode at 0..31"
               & LF
               & "    end moder"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    set_pin: (inout m: moder, n: usize, mode: pin_mode) "
               & "-> none ="
               & LF
               & "        m.pins[n] = mode"
               & LF
               & "    end set_pin"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Indexing such a field with a run-time value is a shift b"
               & "y a computed amount, with a bounds check that stays even"
               & " in `unchecked`. Do it on an image in memory, never stra"
               & "ight on the device ([0740])."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Fine print:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- A packed struct is a raw image, and every bit pattern "
               & "is a valid image. Copying, passing and returning it keep"
               & " all its bits."
               & LF
               & "- Reading an encoded field checks that the bits name a m"
               & "ember and traps if they do not, so a `match` on the resu"
               & "lt can be exhaustive."
               & LF
               & "- Writing a field leaves every other bit alone, and a fr"
               & "esh literal starts from zero. A packed field has no addr"
               & "ess of its own: to update one, pass the whole image."
               & LF
               & "- `layout(packed, u32)` keeps a 32-bit carrier even when"
               & " the highest position would fit a smaller one."
               & LF
               & "- Field widths run from `u1` to `u64` inside packed stru"
               & "cts only; they add no arbitrary-width arithmetic or call"
               & "ing convention."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Exact rules: spec [1870].",
            when 740 =>
               "A device register is read and written by two operations,"
               & " and how the register behaves is data you pass them, not"
               & " a keyword:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- `compiler.register_read(pointer, read_mode)`"
               & LF
               & "- `compiler.register_write(pointer, image, write_mode, r"
               & "eserved_policy, named_mask)`"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The pointer is an ordinary one to `u8`, `u16`, `u32` or "
               & "`u64`, and its width is the one transaction the device s"
               & "ees. Modes and policies are atoms, so a generator can de"
               & "scribe write-one-to-clear, clear-on-read and whatever th"
               & "e next vendor invents without the language growing a wor"
               & "d for each."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    status_raw: (base: usize) -> (raw: u32) ="
               & LF
               & "        port: ptr u32 = ptr(base + 0x04)"
               & LF
               & "        raw = compiler.register_read(port, compiler.norm"
               & "al_read)"
               & LF
               & "    end status_raw"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    clear_events: (base: usize, events: u32) -> none ="
               & LF
               & "        port: ptr mut u32 = ptr(base + 0x08)"
               & LF
               & "        compiler.register_write(port, events, compiler.o"
               & "ne_clears,"
               & LF
               & "                                compiler.write_zero, 0x0"
               & "000_0007)"
               & LF
               & "    end clear_events"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "| argument | values |"
               & LF
               & "|---|---|"
               & LF
               & "| read mode | `compiler.normal_read`, `compiler.clear_on"
               & "_read` |"
               & LF
               & "| write mode | `compiler.normal_write`, `compiler.one_cl"
               & "ears` |"
               & LF
               & "| reserved policy | `compiler.preserve`, `compiler.write"
               & "_zero`, `compiler.write_one` |"
               & LF
               & "| named mask | a fixed number of the register's width, w"
               & "ith a one at every bit a field names |"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A register that cannot be read has no read to perform, s"
               & "o `compiler.no_read` is an error rather than a mode, and"
               & " so is `compiler.no_write`."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "You cannot write a single field of a device register. Fo"
               & "r an ordinary read/write register, read the whole image,"
               & " change it locally and write it back whole. The generato"
               & "r of [0730] writes, beside `control`, `control_decode` f"
               & "rom the raw `u32` to the image, `control_encode` back, a"
               & "nd the constant `control_named_mask`:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    reset_flags: (base: usize) -> none ="
               & LF
               & "        port: ptr mut u32 = ptr(base)"
               & LF
               & "        image: control = (enable: true, mode: external, "
               & "irq_rx: true, of zeroed)"
               & LF
               & "        compiler.register_write(port, control_encode(ima"
               & "ge),"
               & LF
               & "                                compiler.normal_write, c"
               & "ompiler.write_zero,"
               & LF
               & "                                control_named_mask)"
               & LF
               & "    end reset_flags"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    read_modify_write: (base: usize, n: u16) -> none ="
               & LF
               & "        port: ptr mut u32 = ptr(base)"
               & LF
               & "        mut image := control_decode(compiler.register_re"
               & "ad(port,"
               & LF
               & "                                                        "
               & "   compiler.normal_read))"
               & LF
               & "        image.divider = n"
               & LF
               & "        compiler.register_write(port, control_encode(ima"
               & "ge),"
               & LF
               & "                                compiler.normal_write, c"
               & "ompiler.preserve,"
               & LF
               & "                                control_named_mask)"
               & LF
               & "    end read_modify_write"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A fresh local literal knows nothing of what the hardware"
               & " holds. To keep reserved bits as the device has them, re"
               & "ad the image first, as `read_modify_write` does; `compil"
               & "er.preserve` never reads behind your back. That read, ch"
               & "ange and write is not atomic, and it does not carry over"
               & " to clear-on-read or one-clears registers: a destructive"
               & " read consumes state, and a one-clears readback is not a"
               & " command."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A register's reset value initializes nothing. It is what"
               & " the datasheet says the register holds after a reset, ge"
               & "nerated as a constant so that tools and readers know whe"
               & "re they start. The hardware puts it there, not the progr"
               & "am."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The operation, not the pointer's type, makes an access a"
               & " device transaction: there is no `volatile ptr` and no `"
               & "register(t, ...)` wrapper type."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Exact rules: spec [1870].",
            when 1650 =>
               "A hosted program starts at `public main: () -> (code: i3"
               & "2)` in the entry module, and `code` is the exit status, "
               & "passed through the system's C ABI."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The arguments are not parameters. You ask the host for t"
               & "hem, as `io.argument_at(host, 0)` does in ""Reading a fil"
               & "e"" at the top: the host keeps the argument table and len"
               & "ds each argument as a host string ([1660]). The first ar"
               & "gument is the one after the program's own name. C's `arg"
               & "c` and `argv` shape could not be indexed in Landin witho"
               & "ut turning a pointer into a slice, which Landin does not"
               & " do ([0500])."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A Linux executable needs no C library. It starts at `pla"
               & "tform/linux`'s `_landin_start`, which calls `main` with "
               & "the process's arguments; `--link=libc` links the C runti"
               & "me instead. Darwin and FreeBSD always link their C libra"
               & "ry."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "When the program imports `hosted/system`, directly or th"
               & "rough `hosted/blocking`, and so `platform/posix`, the en"
               & "try first calls this C-callable routine with the real va"
               & "lues, before the body of `main` begins, so the environme"
               & "nt is kept beside the arguments and lent the same way:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    void _landin_host_initialize_arguments(int argc, cha"
               & "r **argv, char **envp);"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "When C owns `main` and calls exported Landin routines, i"
               & "t calls that routine itself, with its own `envp` or C's "
               & "`environ`:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- Call it before `blocking.new()` acquires an argument c"
               & "apability, and before starting any thread that may acqui"
               & "re one. File, stream and heap work needs no argument roo"
               & "t, so linking `core/io` alone does not require the call."
               & LF
               & "- The first call with a nonnegative count and a non-null"
               & " table sets the one root; a null environment is an empty"
               & " one. Repeating exactly the same three values is harmles"
               & "s. Using the argument services before the call, or calli"
               & "ng with different values, traps. No ordinary export or c"
               & "allback initializes or resets the root."
               & LF
               & "- Nothing is copied. The C owner keeps the tables and th"
               & "e strings they name readable as long as any world, view "
               & "or callback derived from them can use them."
               & LF
               & "- The arguments start at `argv[1]`, so a C program run w"
               & "ith only its own name gives Landin no arguments."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Freestanding, there is no `main`. The compiler request n"
               & "ames the entry: an infallible ordinary `() -> none` or `"
               & "() -> noreturn` routine, or a naked `() -> none` one, in"
               & " the entry module ([1630] shows the request). The compil"
               & "er's reset initializes RAM before calling it. Returning "
               & "from it, or a failed check, traps, since there is no hos"
               & "ted exit to go to. General build and package orchestrati"
               & "on are outside the firmware path."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Exact rules: spec [1975] for hosted programs, spec [1990"
               & "] for firmware.",
            when 1660 =>
               "The entry point is the usual place to make the program's"
               & " roots "
               & Character'Val (226)
               & Character'Val (128)
               & Character'Val (148)
               & " the host, an allocator, a diagnostics log "
               & Character'Val (226)
               & Character'Val (128)
               & Character'Val (148)
               & " and pass them down as ordinary arguments. The language "
               & "does not reserve that to the entry point: any routine ca"
               & "n call the constructors ([1680]). A hosted root imports "
               & "`hosted/blocking`, whose reads, writes, network calls an"
               & "d sleeps block the thread until they are done; a caller-"
               & "backed one needs only `core/io`. `hosted/blocking` is bu"
               & "ilt over `hosted/system`, which keeps everything that do"
               & "es not wait: the arguments and environment, opening and "
               & "closing files, directories, running programs, entropy an"
               & "d the wall clock. A program that serves several things a"
               & "t once imports `hosted/events` instead, whose waits let "
               & "other fibres run ([1675]); it is built over `hosted/syst"
               & "em` too. A program that never reads, writes or sleeps ma"
               & "y use `system.new()` alone; it is not a world, since it "
               & "cannot read or write."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "This is the essential flow of `examples/derived_hosted`,"
               & " a complete log filter. Its `main` is one line, `code = "
               & "app.entry()`, and the work starts in `entry`, a public r"
               & "outine of the program's own `app` module. That module im"
               & "ports `hosted/blocking`, `hosted/heap`, `core/region`, `"
               & "core/diag` and `core/io`, and `run_logged` is the applic"
               & "ation's routine that does the work:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    public entry: () -> (code: i32) ="
               & LF
               & "        host := blocking.new()"
               & LF
               & "        world: any io.world = any(addr host)"
               & LF
               & "        heap := heap.new()"
               & LF
               & "        mut ledger: region.growing_space = zeroed"
               & LF
               & "        program := region.over(ledger, heap)"
               & LF
               & "        defer region.release(ledger, heap)"
               & LF
               & "        writer: any io.writer = any(addr host)"
               & LF
               & "        mut logger := diag.stream(writer, world.err())"
               & LF
               & "        log: any diag.log = any(addr logger)"
               & LF
               & "        kept: usize = run_logged(program, world, usize(4"
               & "096), log) else (problem)"
               & LF
               & "            _ = problem"
               & LF
               & "            code = 1"
               & LF
               & "            return"
               & LF
               & "        end"
               & LF
               & "        _ = kept"
               & LF
               & "        code = if log.failed() then 1 else 0 end if"
               & LF
               & "    end entry"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Each root is a handle: a small value, copied and passed "
               & "by value, that refers to its state."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- `blocking.new()` and `heap.new()` borrow nothing, beca"
               & "use what they refer to belongs to the process: the argum"
               & "ent root, the descriptors, the last errno and the heap. "
               & "`blocking.new()` acquires the real argument table; it do"
               & "es not create or copy it ([1650])."
               & LF
               & "- A caller-owned provider is made from state the caller "
               & "declares, as the region is made from `ledger`. The handl"
               & "e cannot outlive that state, and the state cannot be rel"
               & "eased while the handle is still used."
               & LF
               & "- An erased capability is built from the address of a ha"
               & "ndle binding, and its dispatch copies the handle from th"
               & "ere. So `world`, `writer` and `host` itself can be used "
               & "side by side, and nothing is locked."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Because the code below the root only sees what it is han"
               & "ded, you can hand it something else. A test gives the sa"
               & "me `run_logged` an in-memory world from `core/io`'s `io."
               & "memory_over`, which takes caller-owned files, arguments "
               & "and output storage and makes no host call, and the run c"
               & "annot tell."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "For that to work, I/O is a concept, not a type, so anoth"
               & "er provider can satisfy it. It travels as `any`, not as "
               & "a type parameter: an indirect call in front of a system "
               & "call costs nothing, whereas an allocator sits in hot loo"
               & "ps and is threaded generically. Same machinery, chosen p"
               & "er case ([1690])."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "It is four concepts, because a provider should supply on"
               & "ly what it has ([1260]):"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "| concept | entries |"
               & LF
               & "| --- | --- |"
               & LF
               & "| `io.writer` | `write`, `write_some` |"
               & LF
               & "| `io.reader` | `read` |"
               & LF
               & "| `io.files` | open, close, `same_file` |"
               & LF
               & "| `io.process` | the three standard streams, the argumen"
               & "t table |"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "`core/io` has more concepts beside these four, each sepa"
               & "rate and not part of the world: `directories`, `environm"
               & "ent`, `programs`, `file_system`, `terminals` and `entrop"
               & "y`."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "`io.world` composes the four for a root that has everyth"
               & "ing, and `world.write` on an erased world reaches the wr"
               & "iter's entry. A UART implements `writer` and nothing els"
               & "e. A diagnostics log asks for `any io.writer`, which is "
               & "why `entry` builds one from the same `host`: an erased w"
               & "orld is not an erased writer, and the two do not convert"
               & ". A routine holding only the world passes it to a routin"
               & "e generic over a writer as it is, and lends a writer to "
               & "erase as an `any io.writer` with `io.writer_of(world)`."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Both providers lend each argument and path as a `text.ho"
               & "st_string`: the bytes the system gave, unconverted and u"
               & "ncopied, so an argument passed on as a path reaches the "
               & "system unchanged, whether or not it is UTF-8. Neither pr"
               & "ovider's arguments include `argv[0]`. The library refere"
               & "nce describes the system providers (`hosted/system`, `ho"
               & "sted/blocking` and `hosted/events`), the memory provider"
               & " and host strings (`core/io`, `core/text`); the exact pr"
               & "ovider contracts "
               & Character'Val (226)
               & Character'Val (128)
               & Character'Val (148)
               & " errno capture, whole and partial writes, retries, `same"
               & "_file` "
               & Character'Val (226)
               & Character'Val (128)
               & Character'Val (148)
               & " are in spec [1975].",
            when 1670 =>
               "When a check fails "
               & Character'Val (226)
               & Character'Val (128)
               & Character'Val (148)
               & " an index out of range, an overflow, a bad conversion, a"
               & "n `unreachable`, a fibre's stack run out ([1675]) "
               & Character'Val (226)
               & Character'Val (128)
               & Character'Val (148)
               & " the program calls a fixed handler that never returns. W"
               & "ith none of your own, a hosted program writes one line, "
               & "`FILE:LINE:COLUMN: trap: KIND`, to standard error and st"
               & "ops; firmware just stops."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "To replace the default, declare a public `panic_handler`"
               & " of exactly this shape in the entry module:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    import core/panic"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    public panic_handler: (kind: panic.panic_kind, site:"
               & " u32) -> noreturn ="
               & LF
               & "        loop do"
               & LF
               & "        end loop"
               & LF
               & "    end panic_handler"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "It gets two scalars and no strings. `kind` is one of `co"
               & "re/panic`'s `out_of_range`, `overflow`, `bad_conversion`"
               & ", `unreachable` and `stack_exhausted`. `site` is a plain"
               & " `u32`, not [1040]'s `site` struct: a number the compile"
               & "r gives the failing operation and its check family. File"
               & " and line live in an optional table outside the target, "
               & "so a small firmware image carries no file names. `refine"
               & " --panic-map` writes that table, and `scripts/source-loc"
               & "ation.py --panic-site`, a script in Landin's source repo"
               & "sitory rather than part of `refine`, resolves a site aga"
               & "inst the build that made it, and only that build."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- The handler must have exactly this infallible form and"
               & " atom domain. A linker symbol of the same spelling does "
               & "not replace it."
               & LF
               & "- A failed check does not unwind: the failed computation"
               & "'s `defer` and cleanup do not run."
               & LF
               & "- Entering the handler a second time traps, including a "
               & "check that fails inside it."
               & LF
               & "- Caller coordinates are a separate value ([1040])."
               & LF
               & "- Hardware faults and naked assembly keep their machine "
               & "obligations."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Exact rules: spec [1960].",
            when 1675 =>
               "Concurrency is a capability, like an allocator or the ho"
               & "st ([1660], [1680]). There is no `async`, no second call"
               & "ing convention and no coloured function type. A routine "
               & "that wants two things in flight asks for something that "
               & "runs them, and whether they really overlap is that provi"
               & "der's answer, not the routine's. Each one runs on a stac"
               & "k of its own, a fibre."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A task is an ordinary routine. It takes its state `inout"
               & "` and fails with `core/failure`'s set, and nothing in it"
               & " knows it runs on a fibre:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    answers: type = struct"
               & LF
               & "        order: [2]u8"
               & LF
               & "        count: usize"
               & LF
               & "    end answers"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    asking: type = struct"
               & LF
               & "        clock: any time.clock"
               & LF
               & "        wait: u64"
               & LF
               & "        tag: u8"
               & LF
               & "        heard: ptr mut answers"
               & LF
               & "    end asking"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    ask: (inout self: asking) -> none ! failure.reason ="
               & LF
               & "        if self.wait > 0 then"
               & LF
               & "            self.clock.sleep(time.milliseconds(self.wait"
               & "))"
               & LF
               & "        end if"
               & LF
               & "        self.heard.val.order[self.heard.val.count] = sel"
               & "f.tag"
               & LF
               & "        self.heard.val.count += 1"
               & LF
               & "    end ask"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A routine starts tasks as a group, over storage it lends"
               & ", and waits for them before it goes on, so whatever they"
               & " borrow from it outlives them:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    ask_both: (runs: any fibre.scheduler, clock: any tim"
               & "e.clock,"
               & LF
               & "               inout heard: answers) -> none ! failure.r"
               & "eason ="
               & LF
               & "        mut stacks: [2 * 8192]u8 = zeroed"
               & LF
               & "        mut asks := try fibre.group_over(runs, stacks, 2"
               & ")"
               & LF
               & "        defer fibre.join(asks) else none"
               & LF
               & "        a: asking = (clock: clock, wait: 20, tag: u8('a'"
               & "), heard: addr heard)"
               & LF
               & "        try fibre.admit(asks, ask, a)"
               & LF
               & "        b: asking = (clock: clock, wait: 0, tag: u8('b')"
               & ", heard: addr heard)"
               & LF
               & "        try fibre.admit(asks, ask, b)"
               & LF
               & "        try fibre.join(asks)"
               & LF
               & "    end ask_both"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- `group_over` cuts the backing into two equal stacks. N"
               & "othing is allocated: the stacks are the caller's bytes, "
               & "and the group comes `from` the provider and the backing "
               & "([0790])."
               & LF
               & "- `admit` waits for a free stack, copies the state onto "
               & "it as a call passes an argument, and starts a fibre that"
               & " calls `ask` with the copy. One group can hold tasks wit"
               & "h different state types."
               & LF
               & "- `join` waits until every task has ended, then fails wi"
               & "th the first failure any of them had. Nothing cancels a "
               & "fibre, so the others run to their own ends first."
               & LF
               & "- A group must be joined before its backing goes. The `d"
               & "efer` does that on every way out, as it releases a regio"
               & "n ([1660]); the compiler does not check that you wrote i"
               & "t."
               & LF
               & "- The state is kept in the group until its task ends, so"
               & " `admit` takes it `escaping into` the group ([0780]). `a"
               & "` may point at `heard`, a parameter of the routine that "
               & "made the group; a local declared after the group is refu"
               & "sed."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Two concepts say what a provider is. A `fibre.scheduler`"
               & " may run each task to its end inside `admit`, before ret"
               & "urning. A `fibre.turns` is a scheduler with `yield`, and"
               & " promises more: a fibre that yields lets every other unf"
               & "inished fibre of its provider run before it goes on."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The blocking host and `fibre.whole` are schedulers. Over"
               & " either of them, `a` sleeps its 20 milliseconds and is a"
               & "nswered first. `stacks.round`, a round of fixed turns fr"
               & "om `core/fibre/stacks`, provides `turns`. Give it a cloc"
               & "k whose sleep takes a turn for each millisecond, and `b`"
               & " is answered while `a` waits:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    turning_sleep: (self: turning, span: time.duration) "
               & "-> none ="
               & LF
               & "        deadline: u64 = self.reading.val + u64(span)"
               & LF
               & "        while self.reading.val < deadline do"
               & LF
               & "            self.runs.yield()"
               & LF
               & "            self.reading.val += u64(time.milliseconds(1)"
               & ")"
               & LF
               & "        end while"
               & LF
               & "    end turning_sleep"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "One `ask_both`, three providers, and the answers are `ab"
               & "`, `ab` and `ba`. So a program written against `schedule"
               & "r` must be right when every task runs to its end as it i"
               & "s admitted. A program that needs turns says so: `fibre.t"
               & "urns_over` takes an `any fibre.turns`, and handing it th"
               & "e host or `whole` is refused where the group is made, no"
               & "t when a task waits for ever."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "On a hosted system the choice is made where the roots ar"
               & "e minted ([1660]), and the routines below never learn it"
               & ":"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "| provider | a call that waits | groups | signals |"
               & LF
               & "| --- | --- | --- | --- |"
               & LF
               & "| `hosted/blocking`, `blocking.new()` | blocks the threa"
               & "d | `scheduler`: each task to its end | none: SIGTERM en"
               & "ds the process |"
               & LF
               & "| `hosted/events`, `events.new(state, entries)` | lets t"
               & "he other fibres run while epoll or kqueue waits | `turns"
               & "` | `core/io/signal`'s `signals` |"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "`events.new` takes a space the caller declares and a rou"
               & "nd's table, one entry more than the fibres unfinished at"
               & " once. A signal is an event a fibre waits for with `next"
               & "_signal`, so only a provider of turns gives one: over th"
               & "e blocking host a fibre waiting for SIGTERM would hold t"
               & "he program before it served anything."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Fibres are cooperative. A fibre gives the core away only"
               & " inside a call to its provider, a `yield` or a call that"
               & " waits, and nothing preempts it. The fibres of one provi"
               & "der are one execution context: between two such calls no"
               & " other fibre of it runs, so a module counter or the last"
               & " error a call left is not raced for. A switch is a full "
               & "compiler memory boundary, and a fibre never moves to ano"
               & "ther thread."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A fibre's stack does not grow. In a program that prepare"
               & "s a stack, a routine compares its frame with the current"
               & " stack's limit before it reserves the frame; the few tha"
               & "t skip the check, small ones that call nothing, fit in a"
               & " margin kept for them. Past the limit, the routine traps"
               & " `stack_exhausted` at its own declaration, before it has"
               & " written below the stack ([1670]):"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    main.ldn:15:1: trap: stack_exhausted"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The limit stands a margin above the stack's lowest byte,"
               & " room for the handler to run in: 640 bytes on x86-64 and"
               & " arm64, 768 on RV64, 384 on Cortex-M0. A program that pr"
               & "epares no stack has no check, and its code is what it wo"
               & "uld be without fibres. How deep a task goes depends on t"
               & "he target: `ask` takes up to 4.4 KiB of its stack on RV6"
               & "4 and less on the others, which is why its stacks are 8 "
               & "KiB. `refine --stack-report=PATH` writes each routine's "
               & "frame and the deepest stack its calls can reach, and nam"
               & "es the `any` calls, recursion and foreign calls that lea"
               & "ve that open. Size a stack from its task's row and the m"
               & "argin. A backing too small for its stacks fails `mem.out"
               & "_of_memory`, where the group is made or when a task is a"
               & "dmitted."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "On one core, nothing runs at once, and fibres there are "
               & "for structure: taking the next request while answering t"
               & "his one. A device answers requests one at a time, in the"
               & " order they arrived, and says `busy` when its queue is f"
               & "ull; two answers are never in flight together. On Cortex"
               & "-M0 a fibre runs on the process stack and interrupt hand"
               & "lers on the main one, so a fibre's backing pays for one "
               & "exception frame, 36 bytes, and the handlers' own frames "
               & "are the main stack's. A handler never switches: it recor"
               & "ds what happened, and the provider looks before it waits"
               & "."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "An application makes a group, and at most a provider, as"
               & " the round above. `core/fibre/stacks` also holds the swi"
               & "tch, `prepare` and `switch` over records a provider keep"
               & "s, for whoever writes a provider of their own, built on "
               & "two `compiler` builtins. A program over `whole` or the b"
               & "locking host imports no switch and runs on every target;"
               & " the switch runs on every target with a native backend. "
               & "A debugger's backtrace through a switch shows `landin.st"
               & "ack_switch`, and a fibre's frames end at `landin.stack_s"
               & "tart`. The library reference describes `core/fibre` and "
               & "`core/fibre/stacks`."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Exact rules: spec [1995].",
            when 1680 =>
               "Landin has no effect system. A routine that does I/O, al"
               & "locates or reads the clock takes the thing that does it "
               & "as a parameter: an allocator, an `io` writer, a peripher"
               & "al handle, a random source. A caller can then hand it an"
               & " in-memory world or a bounded allocator."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The parameter list says what a caller supplies, not ever"
               & "ything the call can reach. Any hosted routine may call t"
               & "he public host and heap constructors itself, and a drive"
               & "r may make a pointer from an address [0460]. Creating th"
               & "e roots in `main` [1660] is a convention the compiler do"
               & "es not check. If you need to know that a call tree does "
               & "no host I/O, read its calls and imports. Exact rules: sp"
               & "ec [1975].",
            when 1690 =>
               "Generic code and `any` share one foundation: evidence th"
               & "at a type satisfies a concept. When the compiler knows t"
               & "he concrete evidence it may specialize the call; otherwi"
               & "se the call goes through a table, and an `any C` value c"
               & "arries that table with it. Static generics and runtime d"
               & "ispatch are two readings of one feature, not two feature"
               & "s ([1310], [1370]).",
            when 1700 =>
               "An atom is identity without payload. Enumerations, error"
               & " sets, variant tags, register encodings and panic kinds "
               & "are all uses of atoms, not separate categories. There is"
               & " no `enum` in the language because there is nothing left"
               & " for it to be.",
            when 1710 =>
               "When a program cannot be written cleanly, ask in this or"
               & "der:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "| ask, in order | then |"
               & LF
               & "| --- | --- |"
               & LF
               & "| can an existing mechanism express it? | a library |"
               & LF
               & "| can the compiler work it out itself? | no syntax |"
               & LF
               & "| must the programmer say it, and does saying it general"
               & "ise or remove another mechanism? | a candidate |"
               & LF
               & "| does it only solve this one case? | not yet |"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A new mechanism should let two old ones leave the buildi"
               & "ng.",
            when 1720 =>
               "Landin makes local checks: origins and escape, consumpti"
               & "on [0910], reference permission [0430], bounds, conversi"
               & "ons and arithmetic. It is not a memory-safe language and"
               & " not a resource-safe one."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "What the checks do not cover:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- Pointer-to-integer conversion and the C boundary leave"
               & " the checked model altogether [0470]."
               & LF
               & "- A copy taken before a `sink` is not refused [0910]."
               & LF
               & "- Distinct pointer or computed-index paths may alias acr"
               & "oss `inout` [0900]."
               & LF
               & "- Two arenas look the same to the compiler [0860]."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "That is a smaller claim than ""safe"" and a larger one tha"
               & "n C's: deliberately unsafe, with static help worth havin"
               & "g. `spec.md`'s guarantee register classifies every obser"
               & "vable failure boundary the compiler implements, and each"
               & " new operation joins it."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Checks are on by default. `unchecked` [1120] removes onl"
               & "y the checks that entry lists, only in the region where "
               & "it is written, and tells the optimizer nothing: it emits"
               & " fewer checks and gives no later pass a fact to assume. "
               & "Optimization adds no undefined behavior, no-alias, overf"
               & "low or floating-point assumption because of it, and keep"
               & "s scalar results and the order of side effects and traps"
               & ".",
            when 1730 =>
               "A successful check can produce a value that stands for w"
               & "hat was checked. A buffer that passed the alignment test"
               & " becomes a `dma_buffer`, and the DMA interface asks for "
               & "nothing else. Distinct types, range subtypes and error s"
               & "ets are enough for this; it needs no feature of its own,"
               & " only the habit."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "For a range subtype, the proof starts with a checked sub"
               & "type value, not with a guard on an ordinary integer.",
            when 620 =>
               "DEFERRED: not in Landin yet; it is planned (Language evo"
               & "lution, in the roadmap). A collection could be stored tr"
               & "ansposed, one array per field instead of one array of st"
               & "ructs, written `soa [4096]entity`, so that one field of "
               & "every element is a contiguous slice. It touches aliasing"
               & ", generics, slicing, `addr`, layout, debug information a"
               & "nd the optimiser all at once, and no program has needed "
               & "it yet. Until one does, generate the arrays with a progr"
               & "am that writes the source ([1540]).",
            when 1740 =>
               "A source file is a possibly empty import prelude followe"
               & "d by declarations in any order. All reached source files"
               & " together are the program. Declaration order does not af"
               & "fect name lookup inside a module [0130]: names are colle"
               & "cted as a set, and a name may be used above the line tha"
               & "t introduces it. Active `linker.library` directives reta"
               & "in canonical source/declaration order for archive resolu"
               & "tion (D202). 'public' rides on a declaration and not on "
               & "a statement [0090]: what a module exports is decided whe"
               & "re the module is written, never inside a body."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    program     ::= import_declaration* declaration*"
               & LF
               & "    import_declaration ::= ""import"" import_path"
               & LF
               & "                           (""as"" identifier | ""("" identi"
               & "fiers "")"")?"
               & LF
               & "    import_path ::= identifier (""/"" identifier)*"
               & LF
               & "    declaration ::= ""public""? (atom_declaration | bindin"
               & "g | function"
               & LF
               & "                                | external_function | ma"
               & "chine_function | linked_binding"
               & LF
               & "                                | type_declaration"
               & LF
               & "                                | concept_declaration)"
               & LF
               & "                    | conformance_declaration"
               & LF
               & "                    | fixed_conditional"
               & LF
               & "                    | option_declaration"
               & LF
               & "                    | tool_directive"
               & LF
               & "    option_declaration ::= ""option"" identifier "":"" type "
               & """="" expression"
               & LF
               & "    tool_directive ::= tool_namespace ""."" identifier"
               & LF
               & "                       ""("" (expression ("","" expression)*"
               & ")? "")"""
               & LF
               & "    tool_namespace ::= ""compiler"" | ""assembler"" | ""link"
               & "er"""
               & LF
               & "    fixed_conditional ::= ""fixed"" ""if"" expression ""then"
               & """ declaration*"
               & LF
               & "                          (""elsif"" expression ""then"" dec"
               & "laration*)*"
               & LF
               & "                          (""else"" declaration*)? ""end"" "
               & """if"""
               & LF
               & "    atom_declaration ::= identifiers "":"" ""atom"""
               & LF
               & "    identifiers ::= identifier ("","" identifier)*",
            when 1750 =>
               "A source file is bytes, a line ends three ways, and spac"
               & "e separates tokens. A span in a diagnostic names a byte,"
               & " and every later stage must be able to point at the same"
               & " one, so the lexical rules are written over bytes. A lin"
               & "e ends at LF, at CR followed by LF, or at a CR that is n"
               & "ot followed by LF, and a file need not end with one at a"
               & "ll. Text inside a literal or a comment may be any UTF-8 "
               & "[0260]; nothing outside them may be. Space is a space by"
               & "te, a tab, a line end or a comment [1780], and any run o"
               & "f them may sit between two tokens. Two tokens whose spel"
               & "lings would run together into one longer token need at l"
               & "east one, which is the whole of the rule: 'mut x' is two"
               & " tokens and 'mutx' is one name. Otherwise space carries "
               & "no meaning, and no rule below this layer mentions it. A "
               & "line end therefore never terminates a statement: when th"
               & "e token after it can continue the expression or selectio"
               & "n before it, it does. This is [1060]'s one-line rule rea"
               & "d from the other direction. One `(` is read by where it "
               & "stands: a `(` that begins a line, and whose matching `)`"
               & " is followed by `:=`, begins [0990]'s destructuring bind"
               & "ing rather than a call of the line above, because a call"
               & " is no place and that reading could never derive."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    space       ::= "" "" | ""\t"" | line_end | comment"
               & LF
               & "    line_end    ::= ""\n"" | ""\r\n"" | ""\r""",
            when 1760 =>
               "Identifiers are lower case, and no identifier is a keywo"
               & "rd. Every name in the language has this one shape: types"
               & ", functions, bindings and fields alike, which is why the"
               & " tour never needs to say which case a thing is written i"
               & "n. Two rules narrow it. A word the keyword rule spells i"
               & "s that keyword and never a name, so 'if' is not availabl"
               & "e as a binding; that one is the tokeniser's. The other i"
               & "s in the rule itself: a name that starts with '_' needs "
               & "something after it, so the lone '_' is the discard of [1"
               & "020] and nothing may be called it. The kernel reserves f"
               & "ifty words; the reserved set of the whole language is la"
               & "rger, and each word joins it when its construct is enabl"
               & "ed in the language. Once reserved, it is a keyword in ev"
               & "ery program, even one that does not use that construct. "
               & "Type names are not among them: u32 and bool are ordinary"
               & " declared names [0120] that the kernel happens to predec"
               & "lare."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "Control words cannot name bindings, types, functions, pa"
               & "rameters, results, fields, atoms, import bindings or loo"
               & "p and block labels. In particular, `begin = 10` and `beg"
               & "in: i32 = 10` are invalid; `(begin)` is not an identifie"
               & "r escape. A longer spelling such as `begin_value` remain"
               & "s an ordinary identifier. D225 records the reservation a"
               & "nd its compatibility consequence."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    identifier  ::= lower (lower | digit | ""_"")*"
               & LF
               & "                  | ""_"" (lower | digit | ""_"")+"
               & LF
               & "    lower       ::= ""a"" ... ""z"""
               & LF
               & "    digit       ::= ""0"" ... ""9"""
               & LF
               & "    keyword     ::= ""addr"" | ""alignof"" | ""and"" | ""any"
               & """ | ""atom"" | ""begin"""
               & LF
               & "                  | ""break"" | ""complete"" | ""continue"" "
               & "| ""dec"" | ""defer"" | ""do"""
               & LF
               & "                  | ""else"" | ""elsif"" | ""end"" | ""escap"
               & "ing"" | ""extern"" | ""fail"""
               & LF
               & "                  | ""false"" | ""fixed"" | ""for"" | ""from"
               & """ | ""if"" | ""import"" | ""in"""
               & LF
               & "                  | ""inc"" | ""inout"" | ""loop"" | ""match"
               & """ | ""mut"" | ""none"" | ""not"""
               & LF
               & "                  | ""or"" | ""ptr"" | ""public"" | ""return"
               & """ | ""sink"" | ""sizeof"""
               & LF
               & "                  | ""struct"" | ""then"" | ""true"" | ""try"
               & """ | ""type"" | ""unchecked"""
               & LF
               & "                  | ""undo"" | ""uninit"" | ""when"" | ""whi"
               & "le"" | ""with"" | ""zeroed""",
            when 1770 =>
               "The kernel's literals are integers, floats, characters, "
               & "quoted text, the two booleans, and contextual `zeroed` a"
               & "nd `uninit`. Integer literals are untyped and take the t"
               & "ype of their context [0190], defaulting to i32 with none"
               & " [0200]; the bases and the separator are [0220]'s. Each "
               & "integer digit run starts and ends in a digit of its base"
               & "; any internal run of underscores separates digits. A nu"
               & "mber ends where its spelling ends: a letter, digit or un"
               & "derscore directly after one belongs to it, so `1u64` or "
               & "`1.5f32` is one malformed literal rather than a number a"
               & "nd a name (D245). `uninit` is D152's restricted private "
               & "inline-array field initializer; it reserves storage with"
               & "out assigning an item image. It has no stand-alone value"
               & " type. `zeroed` has no type of its own: [0540] gives it "
               & "the all-bits-zero image of a directly supplied initializ"
               & "er, assignment or field-label context. D27--D30 establis"
               & "h fixed-array contexts, D39--D43 scalar contexts, D49 an"
               & "d D57--D59 whole array-field and ordinary-struct context"
               & "s, D62 the depth-one indexed field place, D64--D67 label"
               & "led struct fields and static images, D75/D76 variant-bea"
               & "ring struct storage and case payloads, and D87 depth-one"
               & " nested ordinary storage. It remains refused where no en"
               & "abled construct supplies that context. D161 admits [0260"
               & "]'s quoted literal when its direct context is a read-onl"
               & "y `[]u8`; the unescaped source content is UTF-8 [1750], "
               & "and [0270]'s byte escapes are decoded into that view. Ra"
               & "w text [0280] takes the same direct byte-slice context b"
               & "y D164, but interprets no escape and removes a line-lead"
               & "ing closing delimiter's indentation. The maximal opening"
               & " quote run chooses the delimiter width; only a later run"
               & " can close it. Adjacent quotes therefore cannot encode a"
               & "n empty raw literal. Intervening line endings remain con"
               & "tent under D164; empty text uses `""""`. D181 adds `utf8`,"
               & " `utf16` and `cstring` contexts and makes `utf8` the con"
               & "textless default. A scalar escape is encoded as UTF-8 or"
               & " UTF-16 for those views; a byte escape remains exclusive"
               & " to `[]u8`. D162 admits [0210]'s decimal float with [022"
               & "0]'s optional exponent. D166 adds [0230]'s hexadecimal f"
               & "raction and required binary exponent. D167 admits [0240]"
               & "'s type-qualified `infinity` and `nan` values for f32 an"
               & "d f64. D163 admits [0250]'s single-quoted character as e"
               & "xactly one Unicode scalar value of fixed type `u32`. A r"
               & "aw scalar is shortest-form UTF-8; [0270]'s simple escape"
               & "s and `\u{...}` spell scalar values, while byte-only `\x"
               & "NN` does not."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "    literal     ::= integer | float | character | text |"
               & " raw"
               & LF
               & "                  | ""true"" | ""false"" | ""zeroed"" | ""un"
               & "init"""
               & LF
               & "    character   ::= ""'"" (character_escape | unicode_scal"
               & "ar) ""'"""
               & LF
               & "    unicode_scalar ::= any Unicode scalar except apostro"
               & "phe, backslash or line_end"
               & LF
               & "    character_escape ::= ""\\"" (""n"" | ""r"" | ""t"" | ""e"
               & """ | ""\\"" | ""\"""" | ""'"""
               & LF
               & "                      | ""u"" ""{"" hex_digit+ ""}"")"
               & LF
               & "    text        ::= ""\"""" (text_escape | text_byte)* ""\"""
               & """"
               & LF
               & "    text_byte   ::= any byte except quote, backslash or "
               & "line_end"
               & LF
               & "    text_escape ::= ""\\"" (""n"" | ""r"" | ""t"" | ""e"" | "
               & """\\"" | ""\"""" | ""'"""
               & LF
               & "                      | ""x"" hex_digit hex_digit"
               & LF
               & "                      | ""u"" ""{"" hex_digit+ ""}"")"
               & LF
               & "    raw         ::= quote_run raw_content quote_run"
               & LF
               & "    quote_run   ::= ""\"""" ""\"""" ""\"""" ""\""""*"
               & LF
               & "    raw_content ::= any byte+"
               & LF
               & "    integer     ::= decimal | hex | octal | binary"
               & LF
               & "    float       ::= decimal_fraction decimal_exponent?"
               & LF
               & "                  | hex_fraction binary_exponent"
               & LF
               & "    decimal_fraction ::= decimal_digits ""."" decimal_digi"
               & "ts"
               & LF
               & "    decimal_exponent ::= (""e"" | ""E"") (""+"" | ""-"")? de"
               & "cimal_digits"
               & LF
               & "    hex_fraction ::= ""0x"" hex_digits ""."" hex_digits"
               & LF
               & "    binary_exponent ::= (""p"" | ""P"") (""+"" | ""-"")? dec"
               & "imal_digits"
               & LF
               & "    decimal_digits ::= digit ((digit | ""_"")* digit)?"
               & LF
               & "    hex_digits  ::= hex_digit ((hex_digit | ""_"")* hex_di"
               & "git)?"
               & LF
               & "    decimal     ::= digit ((digit | ""_"")* digit)?"
               & LF
               & "    hex         ::= ""0x"" hex_digit ((hex_digit | ""_"")* h"
               & "ex_digit)?"
               & LF
               & "    octal       ::= ""0o"" octal_digit ((octal_digit | ""_"""
               & ")* octal_digit)?"
               & LF
               & "    binary      ::= ""0b"" binary_digit ((binary_digit | """
               & "_"")* binary_digit)?"
               & LF
               & "    hex_digit   ::= digit | ""a"" ... ""f"" | ""A"" ... ""F"
               & """"
               & LF
               & "    octal_digit ::= ""0"" ... ""7"""
               & LF
               & "    binary_digit ::= ""0"" | ""1""",
            when 1780 =>
               "A comment ends a token, and its opener says which kind i"
               & "t is. The three forms are [0010], [0020] and [0030], and"
               & " their openers share a prefix, so length cannot tell the"
               & "m apart: the longest opener decides. '--(' opens a block"
               & " comment, '---' a doc comment and '--' a line comment, a"
               & "nd the form so chosen decides where the comment ends rat"
               & "her than the rule that a token is as long as it can be. "
