               & "openings are the report's `trap`: the handler's, or D361"
               & "'s report calling the C library's `write` off Linux on t"
               & "he stack it was called on, which D491 leaves only in a p"
               & "rogram that prepares no stack.",
            when 469 =>
               "Chosen: both are local function symbols with their sizes"
               & ", `landin.stack_switch` and `landin.stack_start`, which "
               & "Mach-O spells with its underscore and LLDB shows without"
               & " it. No Landin routine can be spelled with the dot, so n"
               & "o routine of a program takes either name; an explicit li"
               & "nk name beginning `landin.` moves the prefix, as one beg"
               & "inning with a local label's prefix moves that. On Mach-O"
               & " each still begins an atom of its own. The start routine"
               & "'s symbol begins at its opening nop, and a prepared firs"
               & "t frame returns past the nop, so the return address less"
               & " one that a debugger looks up lies in the start routine'"
               & "s symbol as well as in its unwind description. Neither r"
               & "outine has a DWARF subprogram, as no routine the compile"
               & "r writes for itself has: a debugger names the frame from"
               & " the symbol and shows no line or variables for it. Their"
               & " unwind information is D447's, unchanged.",
            when 471 =>
               "Chosen: a program whose whole program holds a `compiler."
               & "stack_prepare` checks every ordinary routine's frame in "
               & "its prologue. After the frame record and before the fram"
               & "e is reserved, the routine compares the stack pointer le"
               & "ss its frame with one cell, the current stack's limit, a"
               & "nd when it is below traps `stack_exhausted` at its own s"
               & "ite, its declaration's: through the selected handler or "
               & "the hosted default report, from its frame record, with n"
               & "othing of its frame reserved or written. A call's argume"
               & "nt area larger than the target's unchecked figure (D472)"
               & " is checked the same way before it is reserved, at the r"
               & "outine's site. The cell holds zero on the stack the prog"
               & "ram started on, so routines running there never trap, an"
               & "d a switch installs the resumed context's limit (D472). "
               & "These are not checked: a routine that makes no call and "
               & "whose frame is no larger than the unchecked figure, whic"
               & "h the margin pays for; a naked routine, whose text owns "
               & "the stack; the selected handler, which instead stores ze"
               & "ro in the cell, so that it and what it calls run on what"
               & " a failed check left without a check that would enter it"
               & " again, D232's latch still refusing a second entry; and "
               & "on Cortex-M0 an interrupt handler, which runs on the mai"
               & "n stack that no prepared stack's limit describes: it kee"
               & "ps the interrupted context's limit below its frame recor"
               & "d, zero in the cell while it runs, and puts the limit ba"
               & "ck as it returns. On Cortex-M0 a call-free routine whose"
               & " frame is larger than the unchecked figure keeps the fra"
               & "me record its check needs. A program with no `compiler.s"
               & "tack_prepare` has no check and no cell, and its code is "
               & "byte for byte what it was. The cell is the running threa"
               & "d's (D497). A check is one load, one compare and one bra"
               & "nch, with the instructions that form the frame's address"
               & " and the cell's: three instructions on x86-64, whose loa"
               & "d is `%fs`-relative; six on Linux and FreeBSD arm64 and "
               & "eight on Darwin, which read the thread pointer first (D5"
               & "06); and eight on RV64 and on Cortex-M0, whose branch to"
               & " the trap is a far one.",
            when 472 =>
               "Chosen: `compiler.stack_switch(save, resume)` has two ar"
               & "guments. The switch saves the cell's value in the frame "
               & "it pushes on the leaving stack and, once the resumed sta"
               & "ck is current, loads the value saved in that frame into "
               & "the cell: x86-64 pushes it as an eighth word, a 64-byte "
               & "frame; arm64 stores it at 144 in a 176-byte frame, below"
               & " its frame record (D475); RV64 in the frame's lowest wor"
               & "d, which was unused, keeping 208; and Cortex-M0 pushes i"
               & "t with a scratch word between r4 to r7 and the rest of D"
               & "460's frame, 48 bytes. The switch checks nothing: the ro"
               & "utine that calls it checked its frame, and its save is i"
               & "nside the margin. `compiler.stack_prepare` lays the stac"
               & "k's limit in the same place of the first frame, the slic"
               & "e's base plus the target's margin, and traps `stack_exha"
               & "usted` when the slice cannot hold the first frame and th"
               & "e margin."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The margin pays for what runs below a checked frame with"
               & " no check of its own: a call area or a call-free routine"
               & "'s frame, each up to the unchecked figure; the frame rec"
               & "ord a callee pushes before its check; the switch's save;"
               & " and a failed check's call of the handler or the default"
               & " report, and the handler's own depth; on Cortex-M0 also "
               & "D461's thirty-six bytes, which an interrupt may stack at"
               & " any instruction. The worst before a handler's own frame"
               & "s is two unchecked figures, a frame record and the call:"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "| Target | Margin | Unchecked | Switch frame | Before th"
               & "e handler | Left for the handler |"
               & LF
               & "|---|---|---|---|---|---|"
               & LF
               & "| x86-64 | 640 | 64 | 64 | 152 | 488 |"
               & LF
               & "| arm64 | 640 | 64 | 176 | 144 | 496 |"
               & LF
               & "| RV64 | 768 | 64 | 208 | 144 | 624 |"
               & LF
               & "| Cortex-M0 | 384 | 64 | 48 | 188 | 196 |"
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The default report needs 8 to 24 bytes of that. What is "
               & "left is sized from the depth report's `trap` figure, whi"
               & "ch counts a handler's frame twice (D467), for the corpus"
               & "'s selected handlers that run on an overflowed stack, at"
               & " every profile: those of `runtime/stack-exhausted-reache"
               & "s-the-handler` and `runtime/stack-frame-past-limit-traps"
               & "`, which match the kind and switch away, took at most 38"
               & "4 bytes on x86-64 and arm64 and 544 on RV64, at `none`, "
               & "and 160 on Cortex-M0. A margin of 1 KiB, which covered h"
               & "andlers three times their size, made `runtime/fibre-turn"
               & "s-interleave`'s 4 KiB stacks too small at `none`, and so"
               & " was declined. The report's `margin_bytes` and `unchecke"
               & "d_bytes` give the two figures beside the `trap` depth, s"
               & "o a reader sees whether a program's handler fits; one th"
               & "at does not is refused (D474). The default report always"
               & " fits. A switch between two contexts that were never pre"
               & "pared, both on the stack the program started on, carries"
               & " zero, as that stack's limit is.",
            when 474 =>
               "Chosen: a program whose whole program holds a `compiler."
               & "stack_prepare` and whose entry module selects a `panic_h"
               & "andler` (D232) is refused with `L0508` when the handler "
               & "does not fit the target's margin. What it needs is D472'"
               & "s worst before the handler, 152 bytes on x86-64, 144 on "
               & "arm64 and RV64 and 188 on Cortex-M0, which the report gi"
               & "ves as `handler_entry_bytes`, and the depth report's `tr"
               & "ap` figure, which counts the handler's frame twice for a"
               & " check failing in its own calls (D467); the margin is 64"
               & "0, 640, 768 and 384 bytes. The refusal gives both, the m"
               & "argin and the target, and the handler's deepest path, at"
               & " the handler. A handler whose calls reach an opening of "
               & "D468, `any`, `evidence`, `function value`, `foreign`, `r"
               & "ecursion`, `helper` or `assembly`, has no depth to compa"
               & "re and is refused too, naming the first opening's kind a"
               & "nd routine and pointing at it, with how many more there "
               & "are. The depth is the emitted code's, on the target and "
               & "profile being compiled, so the rule is decided after emi"
               & "ssion and before anything is written, for `--emit=asm` a"
               & "nd `--emit=exe` alike, a dry run included; `--emit=ir` a"
               & "nd `refine check` have no frames and do not raise it, so"
               & " a program `refine check` accepts may be refused when it"
               & " is compiled, and a handler that fits at one profile may"
               & " not at another. A program that prepares no stack is not"
               & " refused, however large its handler: no fibre can exist "
               & "there, and its handler runs on the stack the program sta"
               & "rted on. A program that selects no handler is never refu"
               & "sed: D361's report takes 8 to 24 bytes and Cortex-M0's t"
               & "rap instruction none beyond D461's exception frame, whic"
               & "h the figure before the handler holds, so the default fi"
               & "ts every margin with at least 464 bytes to spare on the "
               & "hosted targets and 196 on Cortex-M0. Off Linux the defau"
               & "lt report calls the C library's `write`, whose stack the"
               & " compiler does not know, on a stack of its own (D491), s"
               & "o the margin pays only its frame. The handlers of `runti"
               & "me/stack-exhausted-reaches-the-handler` and `runtime/sta"
               & "ck-frame-past-limit-traps`, which match the kind and swi"
               & "tch away, need at most 536 bytes on x86-64, 528 on arm64"
               & ", 688 on RV64 and 348 on Cortex-M0, at `none`.",
            when 475 =>
               "Chosen: every FreeBSD link passes `-Wl,--discard-locals`"
               & ", which drops the local symbols whose names begin `.L` f"
               & "rom the image and keeps the global ones, so an explicit "
               & "`.L` link name still resolves and a debugger finds only "
               & "the routines' own names at their addresses. The objects "
               & "keep their labels, as `-L` asks; the image is what a deb"
               & "ugger reads. Linux assembles with GNU as without `-L` an"
               & "d its output is unchanged; on Mach-O no `L` label reache"
               & "s an object and the link drops every linker-private `l` "
               & "name."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "The arm64 switch routine opens with the frame record, th"
               & "en lowers sp by 160 and stores x19-x28 and d8-d15 from t"
               & "he frame's bottom, the stack's limit at 144 and a pad wo"
               & "rd; the frame stays 176 bytes, and a prepared first fram"
               & "e lays the same bytes, its record x29 zero and x30 past "
               & "the start routine's nop. Its unwind rule is x29 plus 16 "
               & "from the record's opening, and the switch points x29 at "
               & "the resumed stack's record before loading that stack's s"
               & "p, so the debugger's frames become the resumed context's"
               & " at that instruction, whether it reads the unwind inform"
               & "ation or the frame record; the rule returns to sp once t"
               & "he saved registers are released. The start routine alone"
               & " is exempt from the contract, by its name: its return ad"
               & "dress is undefined so that a walk ends there (D447), whi"
               & "ch a record would contradict.",
            when 491 =>
               "Chosen: off Linux, in a program that prepares a stack, t"
               & "he report routine pushes its frame record on the stack i"
               & "t was called on, as before, and then moves the stack poi"
               & "nter to the top of a 16 KiB stack of its own in zeroed s"
               & "torage, aligned for C, before it calls `write`. The repo"
               & "rt never returns, so nothing moves the pointer back. The"
               & " frame address still names the record on the failed stac"
               & "k, so a debugger that stops on the terminal instruction "
               & "unwinds to the failed check as before. The stack that fa"
               & "iled pays only the report's frame, which D472's figures "
               & "already count, so the margin and D474's verdicts are unc"
               & "hanged, and the stack report's `trap` in such a program "
               & "is closed while `foreign_write` still says that the C li"
               & "brary is called. A program that prepares no stack keeps "
               & "the report on the stack it is called on, which is the on"
               & "e the program started on, and its `trap` stays open, as "
               & "D468 has it. Linux's report is a system call, which need"
               & "s no stack, and is unchanged, as are Cortex-M0's bare in"
               & "struction and every program that prepares no stack. The "
               & "storage is one per program, which a thread claims before"
               & " it uses it (D498). 16 KiB is ten times what either Free"
               & "BSD guest measured and holds the largest vector state x8"
               & "6-64 saves.",
            when 497 =>
               "Chosen: the cell is per thread. On Linux and FreeBSD it "
               & "is ELF thread-local storage the image addresses in the l"
               & "ocal-exec model, so on x86-64 the check compares the sta"
               & "ck pointer with an `%fs`-relative word. A static Linux p"
               & "rogram sets up its own thread pointer and block: its ent"
               & "ry for the first thread, and the thread facility for eac"
               & "h thread it starts, through `clone`'s `CLONE_SETTLS`. Un"
               & "der `--link=libc` and on FreeBSD the C library sets up t"
               & "he storage of every thread. Darwin has no ELF thread-loc"
               & "al storage: there the cell is the slot of a pthread key "
               & "the program creates before it runs Landin code, which th"
               & "e check reads inline through `tpidrro_el0`, and the prog"
               & "ram's start compares that read with `pthread_getspecific"
               & "` and ends with a report if they disagree. A thread whos"
               & "e cell holds zero never traps, as D471's first stack nev"
               & "er does: the thread a program starts on, and a C thread "
               & "calling into Landin. D472's frames are unchanged; a swit"
               & "ch saves and loads the running thread's cell. A program "
               & "that prepares no stack and starts no thread still has no"
               & " cell and no check.",
            when 498 =>
               "Chosen: the stack stays one per program. Before the repo"
               & "rt moves to it, it claims a latch of its own with an ato"
               & "mic exchange; a thread that finds the latch claimed writ"
               & "es nothing and waits, without returning, for the report "
               & "that claimed it to end the process. The report's frame o"
               & "n the failed stack, and therefore D472's margins and D47"
               & "4's verdicts, are unchanged. Linux's report, a system ca"
               & "ll that needs no stack, takes no latch: each trapping th"
               & "read's line is one `write`. D232's panic latch is the se"
               & "lected handler's and is unchanged.",
            when 499 =>
               "Chosen: each backend lowers an atomic by its ordering, b"
               & "y the mapping of its architecture's memory model: on x86"
               & "-64 a load and a relaxed or release store are a plain mo"
               & "ve, a seq_cst store an exchange, a read-modify-write a l"
               & "ocked instruction with no fence, and only a seq_cst fenc"
               & "e an `mfence`; on arm64 acquire and release accesses are"
               & " `ldar` and `stlr` or their exclusive and LSE forms, and"
               & " fences `dmb ishld` or `dmb ish`; on RV64 the mapping th"
               & "e RISC-V memory model gives, with `.aq` and `.rl` on its"
               & " atomic instructions; and on Cortex-M0 a `dmb` only wher"
               & "e an acquire or a release needs one. Every atomic stays "
               & "a full compiler memory boundary, and the alignment check"
               & " is unchanged.",
            when 522 =>
               "Chosen: the mappings of the C/C++11 orderings the archit"
               & "ectures' authors publish: x86-TSO's and ARMv8's from Sew"
               & "ell's collection (<https://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/~pes20/cpp/c"
               & "pp0xmappings.html>), ARMv7's from the same collection fo"
               & "r Cortex-M0, which has no acquire or release instruction"
               & ", and Table A.6 of the RISC-V unprivileged ISA's memory "
               & "model appendix for RV64."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "- x86-64. Every load is a `mov`; a relaxed or release st"
               & "ore is a `mov` and a seq_cst store `xchg`; exchange, add"
               & " and compare-exchange are `xchg`, `lock xadd` and `lock "
               & "cmpxchg` at every ordering; an acquire, release or acq_r"
               & "el fence emits nothing and a seq_cst fence `mfence`."
               & LF
               & "- arm64. A relaxed load or store is `ldr` or `str`, an a"
               & "cquire or seq_cst load `ldar`, a release or seq_cst stor"
               & "e `stlr`. A read-modify-write's exclusive loop reads wit"
               & "h `ldaxr` when its ordering acquires and `ldxr` otherwis"
               & "e, and writes with `stlxr` when it releases and `stxr` o"
               & "therwise; at `armv8.1-a` it is `swp`, `ldadd` or `cas` w"
               & "ith no suffix, `a`, `l` or `al`. An acquire fence is `dm"
               & "b ishld`, every other `dmb ish`. Darwin and FreeBSD shar"
               & "e it."
               & LF
               & "- RV64. A relaxed load or store is plain; an acquire loa"
               & "d is followed by `fence r, rw` and a seq_cst one also pr"
               & "eceded by `fence rw, rw`; a release or seq_cst store is "
               & "preceded by `fence rw, w`. An AMO carries `.aq`, `.rl` o"
               & "r `.aqrl`, seq_cst taking `.aqrl`; an LR/SC loop, compar"
               & "e-exchange and the one- and two-byte masked loops, puts "
               & "`.aq` on the LR for an acquire, `.aqrl` for seq_cst, and"
               & " `.rl` on the SC for a release. The four fences are `fen"
               & "ce r, rw`, `fence rw, w`, `fence.tso` and `fence rw, rw`"
               & "."
               & LF
               & "- Cortex-M0. A relaxed access is plain; an acquire or se"
               & "q_cst load is followed by `dmb sy`, a release store prec"
               & "eded by one and a seq_cst store has one on each side; ev"
               & "ery thread fence is `dmb sy`, the only option ARMv6-M's "
               & "DMB has."
               & LF
               & LF
               & "A compare-exchange is lowered by its success ordering, n"
               & "ever weaker than its failure ordering (D227), so the acq"
               & "uiring read a failed comparison may ask for is already t"
               & "here. The device, completion and compiler barriers, the "
               & "alignment checks and the panic latch's sequences are unc"
               & "hanged; every atomic remains a full compiler memory boun"
               & "dary, which the IR and the allocators already kept witho"
               & "ut the instructions.",
            when 504 =>
               "Chosen: platform/linux's naked `_landin_start` calls `_l"
               & "andin_start_thread` with the initial stack before anythi"
               & "ng else. That routine walks the auxiliary vector to the "
               & "program headers, keeps what the TLS segment says (its im"
               & "age, the bytes of it that are initialized, its size roun"
               & "ded up to its alignment, and where in a block the image "
               & "and the thread pointer go on this architecture), maps a "
               & "block for the first thread with `_landin_start_map`, and"
               & " returns its thread pointer; an executable with no TLS s"
               & "egment gets its control words in 48 bytes of module stor"
               & "age and maps nothing. The entry makes the pointer the th"
               & "read's: `arch_prctl(ARCH_SET_FS)` on x86-64, `msr tpidr_"
               & "el0` on arm64 and `mv tp` on RV64, the last of which onl"
               & "y a naked routine may write. `_landin_start_main` then l"
               & "ays the block, zeroing it, copying the initialized image"
               & " and, on x86-64, storing the pointer in the word it name"
               & "s, before it holds the processor to its level and calls "
               & "`main`. A routine whose symbol begins `_landin_start` ru"
               & "ns before the pointer exists and is not checked against "
               & "a stack's limit, which on Linux are exactly these; `_lan"
               & "din_start_thread` therefore calls nothing but the map ro"
               & "utine, whose system call it makes itself. For the thread"
               & "s the program starts, the module gives `thread_block_byt"
               & "es`, the bytes a block and room to align it take, `threa"
               & "d_block_alignment`, and `thread_block_lay(block)`, which"
               & " lays a block in the caller's memory and returns the val"
               & "ue `clone` takes with `CLONE_SETTLS`. Under `--link=libc"
               & "` none of this runs: the C library lays every thread's s"
               & "torage and `thread_block_bytes` is zero.",
            when 506 =>
               "Chosen: a program that uses the stack builtins on Darwin"
               & " carries a compiler-owned routine, `landin.thread_start`"
               & ", in its image's initializers (`__mod_init_func`), which"
               & " the loader runs before `main`. It makes the key with `p"
               & "thread_key_create`, keeps eight times the key, the slot'"
               & "s offset from the slots' base, in a word of module stora"
               & "ge, stores a probe, its own frame address, with `pthread"
               & "_setspecific`, requires the inline read and `pthread_get"
               & "specific` both to give the probe, and stores zero again."
               & " A read is `mrs` of `tpidrro_el0`, its low three bits cl"
               & "eared, as the system's own accessor clears them, plus th"
               & "e offset word: a check is eight instructions, against si"
               & "x on Linux and FreeBSD arm64. A key that cannot be made,"
               & " or a read that disagrees, ends the process with status "
               & "127 and one line on standard error, as a processor below"
               & " the executable's level does on Linux. A thread a C libr"
               & "ary starts begins with its slot zero, and so with no lim"
               & "it.",
            when 507 =>
               "Chosen: the latch is one zeroed 32-bit word beside the r"
               & "eport stack, in exactly the programs that have that stac"
               & "k: those that prepare a stack, on Darwin and FreeBSD. Th"
               & "e report exchanges one into it, with `xchg` on x86-64 an"
               & "d an acquire-release exclusive pair on arm64, whose base"
               & "line has no LSE, and moves to the report stack when it r"
               & "ead zero. A thread that read one spins on `pause` or `wf"
               & "e`, writing nothing, until the claimant's trap ends the "
               & "process. The frame record is pushed on the failed stack "
               & "first, as D491 has it, so a debugger stopped in either t"
               & "hread unwinds to its failed check.",
            when 508 =>
               "Chosen: a `link(thread)` datum's location is its offset "
               & "in its module's thread block and `DW_OP_form_tls_address"
               & "`, which a debugger resolves for the thread it has selec"
               & "ted: `x@dtpoff` on x86-64, `.dtpreldword` on RV64 and th"
               & "e variable's descriptor on Darwin. arm64 ELF has no dire"
               & "ctive for the offset in GNU as, and its datum is describ"
               & "ed with no location, as GCC describes one. GDB resolves "
               & "the offset through the C library's thread descriptors, s"
               & "o under `--link=libc` it prints the selected thread's in"
               & "stance and in a static Linux program, which has none, it"
               & " says it cannot find the thread's storage.",
            when 516 =>
               "Chosen: a bodyless `extern(c)` declaration with an expli"
               & "cit `link(symbol: ...)` may name the symbol a naked rout"
               & "ine with a body defines, as a C header declares a routin"
               & "e written in assembly: ordinary code calls the naked tex"
               & "t through the import's C signature, and the text owns ho"
               & "nouring it. The checker admits the pair beside each othe"
               & "r, as it admits C's view of `main` (D279), and the verif"
               & "ied IR's agreement of a symbol's declarations takes the "
               & "pair as one; any other pairing of a naked definition and"
               & " a declaration of its symbol is still L0347. The naked r"
               & "outine is reached through the import, rather than kept a"
               & "s every machine-convention definition is, so a program t"
               & "hat never calls the import carries neither. `platform/li"
               & "nux`'s `clone_thread` is such an import, and its text ha"
               & "s no label: the creator and the new thread run the same "
               & "instructions after the call and each picks its next step"
               & " with a conditional move, or on RV64 a mask, since a hos"
               & "ted body admits no label.",
            when 523 =>
               "Chosen: under `--link=libc`, which Darwin and FreeBSD al"
               & "ways use, each active `linker.library(""NAME"")` reaches t"
               & "he C driver as `-lNAME`, in D202's source order with its"
               & " repeats, after the program's assembly. The driver resol"
               & "ves it as it would for C, a shared library before an arc"
               & "hive of the same name in one directory where its linker "
               & "prefers one. The C runtime's own libraries, `m` among th"
               & "em, therefore link as the driver links them for C: a dyn"
               & "amically linked Linux executable no longer takes glibc's"
               & " static `libm`, whose ifunc members ask the dynamic load"
               & "er for CPU features no static member provides. The defau"
               & "lt static Linux link still refuses the directive (D280),"
               & " and firmware still refuses it (D229). `--dry-run` and `"
               & "-v` show the `-l` operands. This amends D202's archive-o"
               & "nly resolution on every hosted target, and Darwin's `-pr"
               & "int-file-name` query goes with it.",
            when 524 =>
               "Chosen: `linker.directory(""DIR"")` is a tool directive ta"
               & "king one fixed text literal, decoded by the ordinary tex"
               & "t decoder and nonempty. A relative directory is resolved"
               & " against the directory of the file that declares it, so "
               & "a project's `lib` beside its source is found wherever th"
               & "e build runs; an absolute one is used as written. `-L DI"
               & "R`, repeatable on `compile`, `run` and `check`, names on"
               & "e relative to the working directory, as a C compiler's d"
               & "oes. Both reach the driver as `-L` with the absolute pat"
               & "h, before the program's assembly: the command line's fir"
               & "st in their order, so whoever builds can put another cop"
               & "y of a library ahead without editing the source, then th"
